textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t3":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: read each section slowly, underline the <strong>terms in bold</strong> (they are the vocabulary of the exam) and, at the end of each block, try to answer in your own words the question that heads it. The five tables —the degrees of knowledge, the three sources (rationalism/empiricism/criticism), the positions on truth, the theories of truth and the types of science— are the best guide for revising at a glance.</p> </blockquote>\n<h2>1. What can we know? Human knowledge</h2>\n<p>Before we start, a test. Answer quickly: a sandwich and a juice cost €3.20, and the sandwich costs €3 more than the juice. How much does the juice cost? If you got €0.20, you are in good company, but you are wrong: the sandwich would then cost €3.20 and the total €3.40. The juice costs €0.10. The first answer comes by itself and without effort; the right one requires you to stop and check it. In <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a> you will see why our minds work like this and what name psychology has given it.</p>\n<p>Thinking slowly <strong>is costly</strong>. The brain weighs about 2% of the body and uses nearly 20% of its energy at rest, and attention runs out; that is why the psychologists Susan Fiske and Shelley Taylor (1984) described us as <strong>‘cognitive misers’</strong>, who save effort whenever we can. And thinking does not guarantee getting it right either: someone who reasons a lot may devote themselves to finding arguments for what they already believed. So we pile up beliefs we have never examined, and some that, if we stop to think, we know to be indefensible: touching wood, not saying ‘we’re winning’ before the final whistle, not putting your bag on the floor ‘because the money will go’. Many of them we inherit from our culture, which is not always wrong: the anthropologist Joseph Henrich (<em>The Secret of Our Success</em>, 2015) tells how in the Amazon bitter cassava is prepared through a long process whose purpose the people who follow it cannot explain, and which removes the cyanide it contains. Other inherited customs, by contrast, protect against nothing. Should we believe only what we can justify? The mathematician W. K. Clifford (‘The Ethics of Belief’, 1877) answered yes, always; William James (‘The Will to Believe’, 1896), that there are cases in which we have no choice but to decide without sufficient evidence. To put it to the test, look at <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-jersey\">a dilemma about belief and reason</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: write down one of your beliefs that you would not know how to defend if you were asked for reasons. Why do you keep it? Would it cost you anything to give it up? Throughout the topic you will see what separates believing from knowing.</p>\n<p>We spend our lives knowing: we recognise a face in the crowd, we learn to solve an equation, we wonder whether what we see is real or only an appearance. But we rarely stop to think about what exactly <strong>knowing</strong> is. Knowing is, in its most basic form, a <strong>relation</strong>: the one established between a <strong>subject</strong> who knows and an <strong>object</strong> that is known. The <strong>subject</strong> is any person capable of becoming aware of what happens within themselves or around them; the <strong>object</strong> is everything to which we can pay attention: a thing, an experience, an idea, a piece of reasoning. There is no knowledge without these two poles, and much of what we say about truth will depend on the way they are related.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-4\">In order to know we have two great instruments, two “organs” of knowledge: the <strong>senses</strong> and <strong>reason</strong> (or understanding). Thanks to the senses we grasp the concrete objects around us; thanks to reason we solve problems, form concepts and understand reality in a universal and abstract way. Hence two degrees or levels are distinguished: <strong>sensible knowledge</strong>, which we receive directly through the senses, and <strong>rational knowledge</strong> (or intelligible), which goes beyond what the senses offer.</p>\n<p>Sensible knowledge has, in turn, two steps. The first is <strong>sensation</strong>: a psychophysical phenomenon that occurs when a stimulus (light, sound, pressure, temperature) excites a sense organ. Sensations are the “raw material” of knowledge. The second is <strong>perception</strong>, which is not a simple sum of sensations, but an organised and meaningful result, in which our expectations, emotions and previous experience also intervene: that is why we always perceive a figure standing out against a background. The higher step is already rational: the <strong>concept</strong>, the fruit of <strong>abstraction</strong>, is the mental representation of what things have in common, setting aside their individual features (the concept “tree” applies to any tree). With concepts we form judgements and reasoning.</p>\n<p>The branch of philosophy that studies all this —the origin, structure, methods and value of knowledge— is called <strong>epistemology</strong> or <strong>gnoseology</strong>. Strictly speaking, <em>gnoseology</em> deals with knowledge in general and <em>epistemology</em> with scientific knowledge, although today the terms are usually used as synonyms. Paraphrasing the philosopher Max Scheler (<em>Man’s Place in Nature</em>, 1928), we could say that the human being is the only being that knows and, moreover, knows that it knows.</p>\n<p>Depending on how the relationship between subject and object is understood, there are two main positions. <strong>Realism</strong> holds that the object exists independently of the subject and that we can know it: in its <strong>naive</strong> version, it believes that we perceive things as they are; in its <strong>critical</strong> version, it admits that our knowledge is partial and correctable, and understands truth as a goal towards which we advance without ever fully possessing it. <strong>Idealism</strong>, by contrast, holds that what is known does not exist apart from the mind that knows it: for <strong>Berkeley</strong>, ‘to be is to be perceived’. But idealism is not a single doctrine: <strong>subjective</strong> idealism (Berkeley) holds that only minds and their perceptions exist; <strong>transcendental</strong> idealism (Kant) admits that things in themselves exist, but says that we know only phenomena; and <strong>objective</strong> idealism (Plato, Hegel) asserts that the Forms or Spirit are the true reality.</p>\n<p>How does someone become an idealist? By two different routes. The <strong>epistemological route</strong> starts from how we know: when I look at the table, the only thing I have in front of me is my perceptions of it (a colour, a hardness, a smell of varnish), and I never get outside them to compare them with the table ‘in itself’. If that is so, what right do I have to assert anything beyond what is perceived? This is the line of reasoning that, from Descartes’s starting point (the first certain thing is my thinking), leads to Berkeley. The <strong>metaphysical route</strong>, on the other hand, starts from a thesis about what is real: the ground of reality would be something of the nature of thought, whether Plato’s Ideas or the Reason that unfolds in history. The first route reaches idealism through doubts about knowing; the second, through a claim about being.</p>\n<p>The most ambitious form of the second route is the <strong>absolute idealism</strong> of <strong><a href=\"#biografias/hegel\">Hegel</a></strong> (1770-1831), the high point of the German idealism that <a href=\"#biografias/fichte\">Fichte</a> and <a href=\"#biografias/schelling\">Schelling</a> had begun after Kant. For Hegel, subject and object are not two separate worlds that have to be joined: the whole of reality is the process by which <strong>Spirit</strong> (or the Absolute) develops and comes to know itself, in nature, in history, in art, religion and philosophy. That is why no partial knowledge is entirely true: each thing is understood through its place in the whole, and the whole through its history. You will see it in Year 2, in the exploration on <a href=\"#teoria/hf-certeza-sensible\">Hegel and Plato</a>.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The true is the whole.</p><span class=\"per\">— Hegel, <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em> (1807), preface</span></blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th>Level</th> <th>What is it?</th> <th>How is it obtained?</th> <th>Example</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td><strong>Sensation</strong></td> <td>Psychophysical phenomenon; “raw material” of knowledge</td> <td>By the excitation of a sense organ in response to a stimulus</td> <td>Red, cold, a high-pitched sound</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Perception</strong></td> <td>Organised grasp of an object (not the mere sum of sensations)</td> <td>By combining sensations with expectations, emotions and experience</td> <td>Recognising that the red, round thing is an apple</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Concept</strong> (abstraction)</td> <td>Universal and abstract mental representation</td> <td>Through reason, which abstracts what is common and leaves aside individual features</td> <td>The idea “fruit”, valid for any fruit</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<h3>Memory from the inside: levels and types</h3>\n<p>Memory is not a single faculty, but several systems working together. <strong>Sensory memory</strong> holds for an instant what each sense has just captured. <strong>Short-term memory</strong> (or <strong>working memory</strong>) keeps for a few seconds, and with little capacity, what we need right now, such as the figures of a mental calculation. And <strong>long-term memory</strong> is the lasting store, which in turn divides into <strong>declarative</strong> (data and facts: a date, a definition), <strong>procedural</strong> (skills: riding a bike, swimming) and <strong>episodic</strong> (what has happened to us). That is why Locke already tied personal identity to memory: we are, in good part, what we remember being.</p>\n<p>Remembering, moreover, is not reading an intact file but <strong>reconstructing</strong>, and into that reconstruction errors creep. The psychologist Daniel Schacter grouped them into seven, which it helps to split into two classes: those of <strong>omission</strong>, when the memory is lost (transience over time, absent-mindedness from lack of attention, and the blocking of what is ‘on the tip of the tongue’), and those of <strong>commission</strong>, when the memory changes (misattribution of where it comes from, suggestibility in the face of questions or others' accounts, the bias that bends the past to what we think today, and the persistence of what we would rather not recall). Hence <strong>false memories</strong> and even confabulation, and memories implanted by the way questions are put. The lesson for knowledge is twofold: memory is more than enough for daily life, but as evidence —a testimony in court, for instance— it must be treated with care.</p>\n<h3>Can we trust what we perceive and remember?</h3>\n<p>Perception is not a camera that records, but a <strong>filter</strong> that selects and <strong>interprets</strong>. <strong>Attention is selective</strong>: of the huge number of stimuli that reach us, we only process the ones that matter to us at that moment. Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris (1999) asked some students to count the passes made by one team in a video; halfway through the scene, someone dressed as a gorilla walked across the court. Roughly half of them did not see it. This is <strong>inattentional blindness</strong>: looking is not seeing. What is more, the brain <strong>fills in</strong> what it receives: in the Müller-Lyer <strong>illusion</strong>, two equal lines look different in length even after they have been measured. And there are qualities that the subject partly supplies: light is a wave; <strong>colour</strong> is how our visual system translates it according to the lighting it assumes. That is why in 2015 a photo of a dress split half the world: blue and black, or white and gold.</p>\n<p>Before the brain interprets anything, reality has already passed through several <strong>filters</strong>. The first are the receptors themselves, and we do not have five senses but many more. Besides sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch, there are receptors for temperature, for pain (nociceptors), for the position of the body (<strong>proprioception</strong>: that is why you can touch your nose with your eyes closed) and for balance (in the inner ear), and others that report on what is happening inside, such as hunger or thirst. Each one picks up only a band: we do not see the ultraviolet light that guides bees, nor do we hear a dog whistle. The second filter is <strong>thresholds</strong>: a stimulus that is too weak is never felt, and psychophysics, since Ernst Weber and Gustav Fechner in the nineteenth century, measures how much a stimulus has to change for us to notice the difference. And what is felt is processed on three levels at once: the <strong>sensory</strong> (receiving), the <strong>symbolic</strong> (recognising: ‘that’s a dog’) and the <strong>affective</strong> (the same dog delights someone who grew up with one and frightens someone who was once bitten).</p>\n<p>At the beginning of the twentieth century, <strong>Gestalt psychology</strong> (German for ‘form’ or ‘configuration’) studied how the brain organises what it receives. Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Köhler and Kurt Koffka showed that we perceive <strong>wholes</strong> before parts; Koffka summed it up by saying that the whole is <em>something else</em> than the sum of its parts. These tendencies were formulated as laws (Wertheimer, 1923). <strong>Proximity</strong>: desks arranged in fours are seen as groups, even though they are all the same. <strong>Similarity</strong>: in a mixed stand we can tell each team’s fans apart at a glance by the colour of their shirts. <strong>Continuity</strong>: two cables that cross are seen as two lines that carry on, not as four pieces touching at one point. <strong>Closure</strong>: a circle with a piece missing is still seen as a circle, which is why we can read a logo made of separate strokes. Beneath them all lies the <strong>figure-ground</strong> relation: in Edgar Rubin’s drawing (1915) you see either a vase or two facing profiles, but never both at once; whatever acts as the figure seems to be in front and to have a shape, while the ground stays behind, shapeless.</p>\n<p>Another silent job the brain does is the <strong>perceptual constancies</strong>. When a friend walks off towards the far end of the playground, her image on your retina shrinks, but you do not see her shrink (<strong>size constancy</strong>); a door that is opening casts a trapezium, yet you go on seeing it as a rectangle (<strong>shape constancy</strong>); a white T-shirt still looks white under the orange light of a street lamp (<strong>colour constancy</strong>). Without constancies the world would be a chaos of shifting patches. The brain also manufactures <strong>apparent motion</strong>: a film projects 24 still pictures per second and we see people walking; Wertheimer studied it in 1912 with two lights that came on in turn. When this work fails, false perceptions appear, and they should be told apart: in an <strong>illusion</strong> the object exists but is perceived distorted; in a <strong>hallucination</strong> an object that does not exist is perceived, and the person cannot tell it from reality; in <strong>hallucinosis</strong> what is not there is also perceived, but the person realises that it is not real.</p>\n<p>Do we learn to perceive? In 1688 William Molyneux put a problem to Locke, who included it in his <em>Essay</em> (II, 9, 8): a man born blind who can tell a cube from a sphere by touch has his sight restored; will he be able to say, just by looking, which is which? Both answered no: sight would have to learn to match what it sees with what the hands used to touch. For three centuries it remained a thought experiment. In 2011 a team led by Richard Held and Pawan Sinha tested it with children and young people in India who had been operated on for congenital cataracts: straight after the operation they could not recognise by sight the shapes they could tell apart by touch, but they learnt to do so within a few days. Earlier, David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (Nobel Prize, 1981) had shown that if one of a kitten’s eyes is covered during its first weeks, that eye is of little use for seeing even after it is uncovered: there is a <strong>sensitive period</strong> during which experience shapes the circuits of perception. Perceiving is, in part, having learnt to perceive.</p>\n<p>Something similar happens with <strong>memory</strong>: it is not a file that keeps memories intact; rather, it <strong>reconstructs</strong> them. Frederic Bartlett (<em>Remembering</em>, 1932) had English students read a Native American story, ‘The War of the Ghosts’, and asked them to retell it some time later: each version came out shorter and closer to what was familiar to them. We remember through <strong>schemas</strong>: what we already know fills in what we did not retain. Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer (1974) showed a video of a car crash: those who heard the question with ‘smashed’ estimated a higher speed than those who heard it with ‘hit’, and a week later more than twice as many of them ‘remembered’ broken glass that was not in the video. Loftus and Jacqueline Pickrell (1995) reminded some adults of three real episodes from their childhood and one invented one (getting lost in a shopping centre); about a quarter ended up ‘remembering’ the false one. A memory can <strong>blend</strong> with what we hear afterwards and can even be <strong>implanted</strong>.</p>\n<p>Does this mean that we cannot know anything? No: the senses and memory work quite well from day to day. What follows is what <strong>critical realism</strong> defends: our access to reality is partial and <strong>correctable</strong>, and what a single person perceives or remembers cannot be the final criterion of truth. <strong>External verification</strong> is needed: other witnesses, documents, instruments. This weighs on <strong>testimony</strong>: in the United States, in most of the convictions later overturned by DNA evidence there were witnesses, very sure of themselves, who had misidentified the culprit. How certain a memory feels does not guarantee that it is accurate. And it affects <strong>personal identity</strong>, which some philosophers base on memory (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a>): if memories are remade, is who I am remade too?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: look for a very vivid memory from when you were little. Do you remember it yourself, or do you remember what you have been told and the photos you have seen? How could you check?</p>\n<h3>Imagination: seeing what is not there</h3>\n<p>Between perceiving and thinking there is an intermediate faculty that the tradition called <strong>imagination</strong>. Aristotle devoted a chapter of <em>On the Soul</em> to it (III, 3). He called it <strong>phantasía</strong>, a word which, he said, comes from <em>pháos</em>, ‘light’, because sight is the chief sense: it is a movement that sensation leaves in us and that remains when the object is no longer there. This explains why we dream, why we remember (for Aristotle, memory depends on imagination) and why we can call up an image whenever we want, as people did when they learnt a speech by placing each part in an imagined spot. Unlike holding an opinion, imagining is up to us: I can imagine a dragon without believing that it exists. And imagination acts as a bridge towards thought because, according to Aristotle, we always think with the support of some image (<em>phántasma</em>): whoever reasons about the triangle in general pictures a particular one, of a definite size, even though the size does not matter to them.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>That is why the soul never thinks without an image.</p><span class=\"per\">— Aristotle, <em>On the Soul</em>, III, 7, 431a16-17 (our own translation)</span></blockquote>\n<p>The modern empiricists gave imagination a central role and, at the same time, kept it on a short leash. For <strong><a href=\"#biografias/hume\">Hume</a></strong>, imagination is free to separate and join ideas: when we think of a golden mountain, we merely combine two ideas we already had, ‘gold’ and ‘mountain’. It can combine without end, but it cannot invent any material that has not first arrived through experience; that is why someone born blind cannot imagine colours. Kant, for his part, gave it a deeper job: it is imagination that <strong>gathers together</strong> the variety of what the senses supply so that the understanding can think it, a ‘blind but indispensable’ function of which we are hardly ever aware (<em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, A 78/B 103). Imagining is also cognitive work: without imagination there would be no scientific hypotheses or thought experiments (Einstein, aged sixteen, imagined himself chasing a beam of light), no works of art, and no ability to put ourselves in someone else’s place, without which there is no ethics.</p>\n<h3>Knowing that, knowing how and knowing someone</h3>\n<p>Not everything we call ‘knowing’ is of the same kind. You know that Bilbao stands on the estuary of the Nervión and you know how to ride a bike, but not in the same way: the first fits into a sentence that is true or false; the second does not, or not entirely. If someone asks you how you keep your balance, you may not be able to explain it, and yet you do not fall off. The British philosopher Gilbert Ryle (<em>The Concept of Mind</em>, 1949, ch. II), the same Ryle of the ‘ghost in the machine’ (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a>), called the first <strong>knowing that</strong> (propositional knowledge) and the second <strong>knowing how</strong> (practical knowledge). Ryle mocked the ‘intellectualist legend’, according to which every intelligent action consists in first running through some rules inwardly and then applying them: if that were so, applying each rule properly would require another rule, and so on without end. That is why, he concluded, knowing how cannot be reduced to knowing that. A bertsolari, a Basque improvising verse singer, does not look up a rhyming manual while improvising, and someone who knows the rules of Basque pelota by heart cannot return a shot for all that. The debate is still open: Jason Stanley and Timothy Williamson (‘Knowing How’, 2001) defend the opposite view, that knowing how to swim is knowing that a certain way of moving serves for swimming.</p>\n<p>Bertrand Russell (‘Knowledge by Acquaintance and Knowledge by Description’, 1910-1911; <em>The Problems of Philosophy</em>, 1912, ch. V) added another distinction. We know something <strong>by acquaintance</strong> (<em>acquaintance</em>) when it is directly present to us, with nothing in between: the taste you notice when eating Idiazabal cheese, a toothache, your own memory of yesterday. We know it <strong>by description</strong> when we reach it only through features that fit it: ‘the painter of <em>Guernica</em>’, ‘the first person to walk on the Moon’. Almost everything we know about history, science and other people is of this second kind, and rests on testimony. Many languages mark the difference with two verbs: Spanish <em>conocer</em> and <em>saber</em>; Basque <em>ezagutu</em> and <em>jakin</em>; French <em>connaître</em> and <em>savoir</em>; German <em>kennen</em> and <em>wissen</em>. English, unusually, makes do with one verb. You can know almost everything about a person by description and still not know them.</p>\n<h2>2. The sources of knowledge: the senses and reason</h2>\n<p>If we know through the senses and through reason, we may ask which of the two is the <strong>reliable</strong> source of true knowledge. At the beginning of modern philosophy (17th–18th centuries) this question divided philosophers into two opposing currents.</p>\n<h3>Common or vulgar knowledge</h3><p>Not everything we know has been learned in a classroom. A baker knows, by touching it, when the dough is ready; a fisherwoman from Bermeo looks at the colour of the water and the flight of the seagulls and decides whether it is worth going out. It is <strong>common knowledge</strong> or <strong>vulgar knowledge</strong>: that which is acquired through experience, habit and dealings with others, without formal study. It is valuable and often effective. But it has three weaknesses. It is <strong>unsystematic</strong>, because it gathers loose data without ordering them into a theory. It is <strong>poorly justified</strong>: those who have it know <em>that</em> something works, but rarely know <em>why</em>. And it often carries <strong>prejudices</strong> that nobody has examined.</p><p>Scientific and philosophical knowledge are distinguished precisely by their demand for <strong>method</strong> and <strong>justification</strong>: it is not enough that something “has always been said”; one must be able to give reasons and subject them to examination. Let us take two popular beliefs. “God helps those who rise early”: what would we have to measure? We would have to define what “help” means (getting better grades? earning more?) and compare early risers and night owls who are similar in everything else. Or the idea that the full moon brings forward births: it is enough to count births over many years in many hospitals and see if they increase on those nights; studies that have done this with large records have found no such relation.</p><p>However, popular knowledge should not be despised. “Red sky in the evening, good weather in the morning” has some meteorological basis in our latitudes, where the weather usually comes from the west. Sometimes science confirms what tradition already knew; what it adds is proof and explanation.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is popular knowledge false, or does it simply lack systematic justification? Think of something you were taught at home “just because”. How could you check whether it is true?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-11\">The first is <strong>rationalism</strong>, whose great representative is <strong>Descartes</strong>, together with <strong>Spinoza</strong>, <strong>Leibniz</strong> and <strong>Malebranche</strong>. For Descartes the senses cannot be trusted, because they sometimes deceive us (a straight stick looks bent in water). That is why he set in motion his famous <strong>methodical doubt</strong>: doubting everything in order to find a certainty that would withstand any doubt. He discovered that, even while doubting everything, he could not doubt that he was thinking, and on that certainty he founded his philosophy.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>I think, therefore I am. — René Descartes, <em>Discourse on Method</em> (1637)</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Descartes did not merely doubt the senses: he wanted to show what part reason plays even when we know material things. In the second <em>Meditation</em> (1641) he takes a piece of wax fresh from the honeycomb: it still tastes of honey and smells of the flowers it came from; it is hard and cold; it has a colour, a shape and a size; and if you tap it, it makes a sound. He brings it close to the fire and all of that changes: it loses its taste and smell, softens, grows warm, changes shape and size, and no longer makes a sound. And yet nobody doubts that it is the same wax. What, then, was it that we knew about it? Not what the senses picked up, because all of that has changed. Nor what we can imagine, because the wax can take on more shapes than the imagination could ever run through. What remains is ‘something extended, flexible and changeable’, and that is not seen: it is <strong>understood</strong>. Descartes draws the same lesson from what he sees from his window: he says he sees men passing in the street, but all he sees are hats and coats, which might be covering automata; it is his judgement that concludes they are people.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Is it still the same wax? It must be admitted that it is; nobody denies it […]. The perception of it is not sight, nor touch, nor imagination, nor has it ever been, though it seemed so before, but an inspection by the mind alone.</p><span class=\"per\">— Descartes, <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em>, II (AT VII, 30-31; our own translation from the Latin)</span></blockquote>\n<p><strong>The four rules of method.</strong> So as never again to take for certain what is not certain, Descartes imposed on himself, in the second part of the <em>Discourse on Method</em> (1637), four rules. The first is that of <strong>evidence</strong>: not to accept anything as true as long as it does not present itself to the mind in such a <strong>clear and distinct</strong> way that no reason for doubt remains. The other three say how to work: <strong>analysis</strong> (dividing each problem into as many parts as needed), <strong>synthesis</strong> (proceeding in order, from the simplest to the most complex) and <strong>enumeration</strong> (reviewing everything until sure that nothing has been left out). It is the way of proceeding of a geometer, applied to any question.</p>\n<p><strong>Clear and distinct: the criterion of truth.</strong> In the <em>Principles of Philosophy</em> (1644, I, 45–46) Descartes specifies the two words. An idea is <strong>clear</strong> when it is present and patent to an attentive mind, just as we see clearly what is before us in good light; it is <strong>distinct</strong> when, moreover, it is so well separated from all others that it contains nothing that is not clear. A sharp pain is clear, but not distinct: we feel it vividly and yet we mix it with obscure judgements about what is happening in the part of the body that hurts. The 'I think, therefore I am' is the first truth that passes this test, and from it Descartes draws a general rule: everything that I conceive with the same clarity and distinctness will be true.</p>\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, a provisional morality.</strong> Doubt is fine for theory, but life does not wait: one has to decide every day even though one does not yet know with certainty what is best. That is why, in the third part of the <em>Discourse</em>, Descartes gave himself a few rules for living while he rebuilt his knowledge, just as someone who pulls down his house to build it again looks for another in which to stay during the works: to obey the laws and customs of his country, guided by the most moderate opinions; to remain firm in a decision once taken, like the traveller lost in a forest who does not change direction at every step, because at least in that way he will get somewhere; to try to conquer himself rather than fortune, and to change his desires rather than the order of the world; and to devote his life to cultivating reason. You will read the <em>Discourse</em> in Year 2, because it is one of the PAU texts, in the topic on <a href=\"#teoria/hf-racionalismo\">Descartes and rationalism</a>.</p>\n<p>For rationalism, the true source of knowledge is <strong>reason</strong>, which finds in itself <strong>innate ideas</strong> —clear and evident principles that do not come from experience— from which it can reach reality.</p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"#biografias/leibniz\">Leibniz</a></strong> (1646-1716) gave the clearest reason why the senses are not enough. The senses only offer <strong>examples</strong>, particular truths; and however many examples we gather, they never prove that something has to be so <strong>always and necessarily</strong>. You can measure with a protractor the angles of twenty triangles drawn in your exercise book and you will get about 180° (give or take a degree or so); but that in every triangle on the plane they add up to exactly 180°, including the ones nobody has drawn, you do not know by measuring, but through a proof. The senses are needed to awaken that knowledge, but they are not its foundation. Leibniz did not believe in a mind full of ready-made ideas, but in something in between: a disposition, like the veins in a block of marble that favour a figure before the sculptor brings it out. And in this way he corrected the empiricists’ motto: ‘there is nothing in the mind that has not first passed through the senses… <strong>except the mind itself</strong>’ (<em>New Essays on Human Understanding</em>, written in 1704 and published in 1765). More in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-racionalismo\">‘Rationalism and empiricism’</a>.</p>\n<p>The second current is <strong>empiricism</strong>, represented by <strong>Locke</strong> and <strong>Hume</strong>. It holds exactly the opposite: there are no innate ideas; the mind at birth is like a <strong>“blank sheet”</strong> (<em>tabula rasa</em>) that gradually fills with what it receives. All our knowledge comes from <strong>experience</strong>, which is at once its origin and its limit: we cannot know anything that has not first passed through the senses. Hume took this idea very far, even claiming that scientific knowledge is only <strong>probable</strong>, since it is based on the habit of observing that certain facts repeat themselves. Hume does not deny science: he shows that its foundation is not rational but psychological, <strong>custom</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"#biografias/locke\">Locke</a></strong> opened Book II of his <em>Essay Concerning Human Understanding</em> (1690) with the decisive question: if there are no innate ideas, where does the mind get everything it contains? Curiously, he does not use the Latin phrase <em>tabula rasa</em> there, but ‘white paper’, and he answers in a single word: <strong>experience</strong>. But for him experience has two branches: <strong>sensation</strong>, which gives us the ideas of external things (yellow, hot, hard, bitter), and <strong>reflection</strong>, the mind’s perception of its own operations (thinking, doubting, believing, willing). With these materials, the <strong>simple ideas</strong>, the mind builds <strong>complex ideas</strong> by combining them, such as ‘apple’, ‘army’ or ‘gratitude’. Reason is not cancelled out: it works, compares and composes, but only with what has reached it.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Let us then suppose the mind to be, as we say, white paper, void of all characters, without any ideas: how comes it to be furnished? […] Whence has it all the materials of reason and knowledge? To this I answer, in one word, from experience.</p><span class=\"per\">— Locke, <em>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</em> (1690), II, 1, 2 (original text)</span></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hume</strong> sharpened the vocabulary. He calls everything in the mind <strong>perceptions</strong> and divides them into two classes according to their force and liveliness: <strong>impressions</strong>, what we feel when we see, hear, love or hate, and <strong>ideas</strong>, the fainter copies left over when we remember or imagine. Feeling the icy water at La Concha beach in January is not the same as remembering it in August. From this comes his <strong>copy principle</strong>: every simple idea derives from an impression. And from this, a critical tool: when faced with a suspicious philosophical word, ask which impression its idea comes from; if none turns up, the word probably means nothing. Hume would use this test to examine causation and the self. Hume himself flagged an exception: someone who had seen every shade of blue but one could, he thought, imagine the missing shade without ever having seen it.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The most lively thought is still inferior to the dullest sensation.</p><span class=\"per\">— Hume, <em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em>, II (original text)</span></blockquote>\n<p>The synthesis was offered by <strong>Kant</strong> with what he called <strong>criticism</strong> (or transcendental idealism). Kant saw that both currents were partly right and that the two sources need and complete each other: the senses provide the content (the impressions) and the understanding provides the forms and categories that order them. But this is not a simple middle point; it is what Kant himself compared to a <strong>Copernican revolution</strong>: it is not knowledge that conforms to objects, but objects that conform to the forms of our mind (preface to the 2nd edition of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, 1787). We know, then, by combining experience and reason, but we can know reality only as it appears to us, not things “in themselves”.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind. — Immanuel Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781)</p> </blockquote>\n<table data-bloque=\"fil-t3-17\">\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Rationalism</strong></th> <th><strong>Empiricism</strong></th> <th><strong>Criticism</strong></th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>What does it trust?</td> <td><strong>Reason</strong></td> <td><strong>Experience</strong> (the senses)</td> <td><strong>Both</strong>, which complete each other</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Authors</td> <td>Descartes, Spinoza, Leibniz, Malebranche</td> <td>Locke, Hume</td> <td>Kant</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Key idea</td> <td>There are <strong>innate ideas</strong>; the senses deceive</td> <td>The mind is a <strong>blank sheet</strong>; there are no innate ideas</td> <td>We know by joining what the senses and the understanding provide</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Limit of knowing</td> <td>Reason, well used, reaches reality</td> <td>We cannot go beyond experience</td> <td>We do not know things “in themselves”, only as they appear to us</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p><strong>Two ways of clearing the mind.</strong> Before building, Descartes and Bacon, a forerunner of empiricism, wanted to clear the ground. In the reading <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-descartes-bacon\">‘Descartes and Bacon: doubt and the idols’</a> you follow Descartes through the first two <em>Meditations</em>, from the stick that looks broken in water to the dream and the evil demon, and Bacon through the <em>Novum Organum</em>, which catalogues the ‘idols’ or prejudices that distort the understanding and proposes working like the bee, which gathers and transforms, and not like the ant, which only accumulates, nor like the spider, which spins everything out of itself. If radical doubt seems like a game to you, the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-cubeta\">‘The brain in a vat’</a> asks you to decide how you would live without being able to rule it out.</p>\n<p>Who won the dispute? Psychology and neuroscience have not settled it: they have moved it. On the innatist side, Noam Chomsky argued that children learn their language too quickly and from too little data to be starting from scratch, and Elizabeth Spelke has shown that babies only a few months old already expect an object to go on existing when it is hidden and not to pass through a wall. On the empiricist side, artificial neural networks learn to recognise faces or write texts purely from enormous numbers of examples, and the plasticity of the brain (remember Hubel and Wiesel’s kittens) shows how much it depends on experience. Hardly anyone today defends a mind full of ready-made ideas or a completely blank sheet: the discussion is about how much the mind comes with as standard and how much it learns.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose something you know how to do really well (playing an instrument, cooking a Spanish omelette, dribbling past someone in football). Could you write it all down so that someone else could learn it just by reading? If you cannot, is it any less knowledge than what is in your class notes? And where did you get it from: from experience, from something you already had in you, or from both?</p>\n<h3>Dictionary: faculties and sources of knowledge</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Proprioception</td><td>The sense of the position and movement of one’s own body, thanks to receptors in the muscles, tendons and joints.</td></tr><tr><td>Threshold</td><td>The minimum intensity a stimulus needs in order to be felt, or the minimum difference needed for us to notice a change.</td></tr><tr><td>Gestalt</td><td>A school of psychology that studied how we perceive organised wholes rather than separate pieces.</td></tr><tr><td>Figure-ground</td><td>The organisation by which one part of what is perceived stands out with a shape while the rest stays behind, shapeless.</td></tr><tr><td>Perceptual constancy</td><td>The tendency to perceive the size, shape and colour of objects as stable even when what reaches the senses changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Hallucination / hallucinosis</td><td>Perception without an object: in hallucination it cannot be told apart from reality; in hallucinosis, it can.</td></tr><tr><td><em>Phantasía</em></td><td>For Aristotle, imagination: the capacity to keep and produce images when the object is not present.</td></tr><tr><td>Knowing that / knowing how</td><td>Knowledge expressed in true or false propositions / practical knowledge, shown in doing (Ryle).</td></tr><tr><td>By acquaintance / by description</td><td>Knowledge through direct contact with something / knowledge through features that fit only that thing (Russell).</td></tr><tr><td>Sensation / reflection</td><td>In Locke, the two sources of ideas: experience of external things and experience of the mind’s own operations.</td></tr><tr><td>Impression / idea</td><td>In Hume, the lively perception of feeling / its fainter copy in memory or imagination.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"podemos-alcanzar-la-verdad-dogmatismo-escepticismo-y-criticismo\">3. Can we reach the truth? Dogmatism, scepticism and criticism</h2>\n<p>It is one thing to ask <em>with what</em> we know and another, more radical, to ask whether we really <em>can</em> know. Faced with the possibilities of human knowledge, the history of philosophy has tried out four main positions.</p>\n<p><strong>Dogmatism</strong> is the attitude of someone who fully trusts the capacity of reason to reach the truth without any doubt. It believes that we can know the essence of things and arrive at <strong>absolute and unchanging truths</strong>. It is a firm position, but it runs the risk of naivety: it takes the capacity to know for granted without examining it first. Beware: in <strong>Kant</strong>, ‘dogmatic’ also has a technical sense. He gives that name to the metaphysics prior to criticism, which uses reason without first examining its limits; this is not the same as the naive attitude of someone who never doubts.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-20\">At the opposite extreme is <strong>scepticism</strong>, which casts doubt on —or even denies— that the human being can attain certain and secure knowledge. The ancient scepticism of <strong>Pyrrho of Elis</strong> and <strong>Sextus Empiricus</strong>, however, did not deny that one could know: since it found no definitive criterion for deciding between opposing opinions, it <strong>suspended judgement</strong> (<em>epokhê</em>) and thus sought peace of mind (<em>ataraxia</em>). It was, above all, a practical attitude, a way of living. Beware, by contrast, of total scepticism: it turns against itself, because to claim “nothing can be known” is already a categorical claim about what we can know (that is why the Pyrrhonists avoided asserting it).</p>\n<p><strong>Pyrrho: living without opinions.</strong> <a href=\"#biografias/pirron\">Pyrrho of Elis</a> (c. 360-270 BC) travelled with Alexander the Great as far as India. He wrote nothing: we know him through his pupil <a href=\"#biografias/timon\">Timon of Phlius</a>. According to Timon, anyone who wants to be happy must ask three questions: what things are like by nature, what attitude to take towards them, and what they will gain from that attitude.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Things are equally indifferent, unmeasurable and undecidable (<em>adiáphora, astáthmeta, anepíkrita</em>); so we should trust neither our senses nor our opinions, but remain without opinion, leaning to no side and unshaken, saying of each thing that it no more is than is not. Whoever is so disposed will first gain the habit of asserting nothing (<em>aphasía</em>), and then serenity (<em>ataraxia</em>). <span class=\"per\">— Timon, as reported by Aristocles, in Eusebius, <em>Preparation for the Gospel</em> XIV 18, 2-4 (my own abridged translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>Five centuries later, the physician <strong>Sextus Empiricus</strong> (2nd century AD) gathered this tradition in his <em>Outlines of Pyrrhonism</em> and opened it with a distinction that organises this whole section: anyone looking for something either finds it, or declares that it cannot be found, or keeps looking. The <strong>dogmatists</strong> (Aristotle, Epicurus, the Stoics) believe they have found the truth; <strong>Academics</strong> such as Carneades claim it cannot be reached, which for Sextus is also a dogma, though a negative one; the <strong>sceptics</strong> keep looking. Hence their name: <em>skeptikós</em> does not mean ‘one who doubts’ but ‘one who examines’.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Scepticism is the ability to set against each other, in any way whatever, what appears and what is thought; through it, because of the equal force (<em>isosthéneia</em>) of the opposed things and arguments, we come first to suspension of judgement (<em>epojé</em>) and then to tranquillity (<em>ataraxia</em>). <span class=\"per\">— Sextus Empiricus, <em>Outlines of Pyrrhonism</em> I 8 (my own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>To produce this balance of forces they used standard arguments, the <strong>modes</strong> (<em>trópoi</em>). The <strong>ten modes</strong> that tradition attributes to <strong>Aenesidemus</strong> (1st century BC) show that one and the same thing appears differently depending on the animal (sea water kills people and keeps fish alive), the person, the sense, the condition (to someone with jaundice, honey tastes bitter), position and distance (from afar, a square tower looks round), mixtures, quantity, relation, frequency (a comet amazes us; the sun, which is more amazing, does not) and the customs of each people. The <strong>five modes of Agrippa</strong> look at arguments: <strong>disagreement</strong>, <strong>infinite regress</strong>, <strong>relativity</strong>, <strong>hypothesis</strong> and <strong>circularity</strong>. Three of them make up the trilemma in section 4.</p>\n<p>How does calm arrive? The painter <strong>Apelles</strong> could not manage to paint the foam on a horse’s mouth and, fed up, threw the sponge he used to clean his brushes at the picture: the mark it left was perfect foam (Sextus, I 28). The same happens to the sceptic: he did not reach calm by deciding who was right; he suspended judgement and calm came of its own accord. And how does someone who asserts nothing live? By following what <strong>appears</strong>: he eats when he is hungry, respects the laws of his city and learns a trade; he does not say ‘honey is sweet’ but ‘honey seems sweet to me’. <a href=\"#biografias/hume\">Hume</a> was not convinced: the Pyrrhonist wins arguments, but as soon as he returns to ordinary life, ‘nature is always too strong for principle’. The Pyrrhonist would reply that this is exactly what he did: let life decide where reason cannot.</p>\n<p>The most famous answer to the sceptics came from someone who had been one of them. <a href=\"#biografias/agustin\">Augustine of Hippo</a> (354-430) came to believe, with the Academics, that nothing can be known; after his conversion he wrote <em>Against the Academics</em> (386). He looked for a truth that no doubt could touch and found it in the very person who doubts: whoever doubts knows that they are alive, remembers what they are doubting, wants to be certain and knows that they do not know (<em>On the Trinity</em> X 10, 14). Not even error threatens it:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>If I am mistaken, I exist. For someone who does not exist cannot be mistaken either; therefore, if I am mistaken, I exist. <span class=\"per\">— Augustine, <em>The City of God</em> XI 26: <em>si enim fallor, sum</em> (my own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>Twelve centuries later, <strong>Descartes</strong> reached a very similar certainty, the ‘I think, therefore I am’ you already know, and the resemblance was pointed out to him. He replied (letter to Colvius, 1640) that Augustine used the idea for something else (to find an image of the Trinity in the soul), whereas he made it the foundation of all science. There is another difference: the Pyrrhonist doubts in order to live calmly and goes on doubting; Descartes doubts <strong>once in his life</strong>, deliberately and thoroughly, in order to stop doubting. His doubt is a <strong>method</strong>, not a way of life.</p>\n<p>Between the two stands <strong>relativism</strong> (and its more personal form, <strong>subjectivism</strong>), which denies that there are universal truths valid for everyone: each truth would depend on the subject, society or culture that holds it. Its first defender was the sophist <strong>Protagoras</strong>, with a famous sentence:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>The human being is the measure of all things. — Protagoras</p> </blockquote>\n<p>It has a valuable side (it invites tolerance and respect for other points of view), but taken to the extreme it is problematic: if everything is equally valid, it becomes impossible to criticise any lie or any injustice.</p>\n<p>Against Protagoras, Plato developed a celebrated objection in the <em>Theaetetus</em>: relativism falls into <strong>self-contradiction</strong>. If we accept that ‘man is the measure of all things’ as a universal truth, then there is already at least one non-relative statement; and if it holds only for Protagoras, then those who think the opposite are equally right, and his position loses all force. Hence the need to distinguish between the fact that our beliefs are conditioned by culture and the claim that <strong>truth</strong> itself depends on it.</p>\n<p>Relativism also has a <strong>social</strong> version, which places the measure not in each individual, but in each society. Its starting point was the work of the French sociologist <strong>Émile Durkheim</strong> (1858-1917). In <em>The Elementary Forms of Religious Life</em> (1912) he argued that even our most basic categories —time, space, cause, class— arise from life in common: we divide time according to the rhythms of society (for you, the year begins in September, with the school year, and is measured in assessment terms and holidays) and we classify things in the image of how people group together. Durkheim did not mean by this that everything is arbitrary; he believed in the objectivity of science. But others drew the relativist conclusion: if each society has its own categories, there would be no way of thinking better than another. The objection is the one you have just seen with Plato, plus a distinction: knowing <strong>where</strong> an idea comes from does not decide <strong>whether it is true</strong>. The calendar is a convention; that the Earth takes about 365 days to go round the Sun is not.</p>\n<p>Relative to what? <strong>Cognitive relativism</strong> holds that the truth or justification of what we believe depends on a framework (a person, a theory, a culture), with no neutral standpoint between frameworks. <strong>Cultural relativism</strong> applies this to the customs of each people (you saw it with ethnocentrism in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a>) and <strong>moral relativism</strong> to what is good and just (it will return in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>). Careful: not everything that depends on a point of view is relativism. ‘The bus stop is on the left’ is true for someone leaving school and false for someone arriving, but once you fix where you are looking from, it is true for everyone. Against cognitive relativism, Plato wrote:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>And the most exquisite part of all (<em>to kompsótaton</em>): Protagoras, by granting that whatever each person believes is true, admits that the opinion of those who think he is wrong is true. <span class=\"per\">— Plato, <em>Theaetetus</em> 171a (my own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>In the dialogue, Protagoras replies that some opinions are not truer than others but <strong>better</strong>, more beneficial, and that the wise person, like a doctor, replaces worse opinions with better ones; but then someone must be able to judge which is better. One way out remains: ‘my thesis is only true <em>for me</em>’. It then stops being a theory of knowledge and becomes a piece of news about what one person believes, binding on no one (M. Burnyeat, 1976).</p>\n<p>The fourth position is Kant’s <strong>criticism</strong>, which we already know: neither blindly trusting reason (dogmatism) nor distrusting it completely (scepticism), but carefully <strong>examining</strong> its possibilities and its limits before pronouncing. It has good reasons in its favour, but it is one position among others, not the only defensible one. In this attitude of examination we recognise that of Socrates, who made awareness of his own ignorance the starting point of all knowledge. Tradition sums it up in a famous sentence, although in Plato’s <em>Apology</em> Socrates says, more exactly, that he does not think he knows what he does not know.</p>\n<h3>When the senses and reason deceive each other</h3><p>Blaise Pascal (1623-1662), mathematician and philosopher, devoted several pages of his <em>Thoughts</em> (published after his death in 1670) to what he called the “deceptive powers” of the human being, with the imagination at their head. His diagnosis is disturbing: the senses and reason, our two sources of truth, not only fail each on their own, but deceive <strong>one another</strong>. The senses offer false appearances to reason; and reason returns the favour, because the passions of the soul in turn alter what the senses perceive.</p><p>Both directions are seen in a single day. In the morning you put a stick into the river water and it looks broken: reason, which knows something of refraction, corrects the senses. But that night, as you return alone along a dark corridor, fear turns the coat hanging on the door into a figure waiting for you. There it is not the senses that deceive reason, but a passion that fabricates what you believe you see.</p><p>Much earlier, Plato had taken this mistrust to an unforgettable image: the <strong>Allegory of the cave</strong> (<em>Republic</em>, Book VII, 514a-517a). Some prisoners, chained since childhood facing a wall, only see the shadows of objects that others carry before a fire, and take those shadows for reality. If one is freed and climbs towards the exit, the light hurts at first; little by little he distinguishes the things themselves and, finally, the sun. The ascent represents the path from ignorance to knowledge, and the sun, the <strong>Idea of the Good</strong>, which makes everything else visible and comprehensible.</p><p>Are there caves today? Whoever only gets information from videos recommended by an app lives, in a way, before a wall of shadows chosen for him, and may take “the world” to be what is a selection.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what chains bind us today? Would we recognise them, or would they seem to us, as to the prisoners, normal?</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“I know only that I know nothing.” — attributed to Socrates</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Kant does not present criticism as a middle way but as the third act of a story. In the preface to the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781) he tells the story of metaphysics as a war: the dogmatists build empires of assertions; the sceptics, ‘a kind of nomads who abhor all permanent cultivation of the soil’, knock them down from time to time, and reason ends up weary. The way out is a <strong>tribunal</strong> in which reason judges itself. That is why Kant does not despise the sceptic: he calls him the ‘taskmaster’ of the dogmatic reasoner.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The first step in matters of pure reason, the one that marks its childhood, is dogmatic. The second is sceptical and shows the caution of a judgement made wiser by experience. But a third step is still needed, which belongs to mature judgement: to submit to examination not the facts of reason but reason itself, with all its power. <span class=\"per\">— Kant, A 761 / B 789 of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (my own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<table data-bloque=\"fil-t3-27\">\n<thead>\n<tr> <th>Position</th> <th>Can we know the truth?</th> <th>Risk or contribution</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td><strong>Dogmatism</strong></td> <td>Yes, fully; there are absolute truths</td> <td>Naivety: it does not examine its own capacity to know</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Scepticism</strong></td> <td>No, or at least not with certainty</td> <td>It contradicts itself if it denies everything; it contributes caution</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Relativism / subjectivism</strong></td> <td>Only truths relative to the subject or the culture</td> <td>It encourages tolerance, but if “anything goes” there is no room for criticism</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Criticism</strong></td> <td>Yes, but within limits, examining reason and experience</td> <td>It examines before asserting; its cost: things “in themselves” remain beyond our reach</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p>Greek scepticism was not just an attitude: in the very <strong>Academy</strong> that Plato founded, centuries later, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/carneades\">Carneades</a></strong> (214-129 BC) turned it into a method. He held that we cannot reach <strong>absolute certainties</strong>, but that this does not condemn us to paralysis: to live and act it is enough to be guided by the <strong>probable</strong>, by what seems reasonable and is confirmed when we examine it. His exact word was <strong>the persuasive</strong> (<em>pithanón</em>), what seems true to us; Cicero translated it into Latin as <em>probabile</em>, hence the summary ‘being guided by the probable’. According to the account given by <strong>Sextus Empiricus</strong> (<em>Adversus mathematicos</em> VII 166-189), Carneades distinguished <strong>three degrees</strong> in this criterion: an impression may be merely <strong>persuasive</strong>; <strong>persuasive and uncontradicted</strong> (<em>aperíspastos</em>), when none of the impressions that accompany it contradicts it; or <strong>persuasive, uncontradicted and thoroughly examined</strong> (<em>diexodeuméne</em>), when in addition each accompanying impression has been checked one by one. The more that is at stake, the higher the degree that must be demanded. Sent to Rome as an ambassador (155 BC), one day he gave a brilliant speech in favour of justice and, the next day, another equally brilliant one against it, to show that almost every thesis has its reply. He left nothing in writing: we know him through his disciples.</p>\n<p><strong>An example of the three degrees.</strong> From the other side of the street you see someone at the bus stop with your friend Ane’s rucksack: it is <strong>persuasive</strong> that it is her. If in addition she has her hair and her way of moving, it is the stop where you wait for her every morning and nothing seems out of place, the impression is <strong>persuasive and uncontradicted</strong>. That is enough for waving to her; but if you are going to give her the keys to your house, you cross over, look at her closely and speak to her: now you have <strong>examined it thoroughly</strong>. None of the three degrees gives absolute certainty (she could have a twin sister), but each one allows you to act with more reasons than the one before.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if good reasons can be given for and against almost anything, how do you decide? Is “the most probable” enough as a criterion for acting, even if it is not certain? In Year 2 you will see the whole sceptical tradition; you can place Carneades in the <a href=\"#genealogias/esc\">“Scepticism”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<p>That same prudence received, already in the Renaissance, a memorable name from the cardinal <strong><a href=\"#biografias/nicolas_cusa\">Nicholas of Cusa</a></strong> (1401-1464): <strong>learned ignorance</strong>. His idea is that true knowledge begins by recognising the <strong>measure of what we do not know</strong>. Finite reason cannot grasp the infinite (ultimate truth, God) exactly; it can only <strong>approach</strong> it, as a polygon with ever more sides approaches the circle without ever becoming one.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>The more deeply someone is learned in this ignorance, the closer they will come to the truth. — Nicholas of Cusa, <em>Learned Ignorance</em> (1440), I, 3</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Protagoras was not the only famous sophist. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/hipias\">Hippias of Elis</a></strong> (c. 460-c. 400 BC) was a <strong>polymath</strong>: he boasted of knowing almost everything —mathematics, astronomy, grammar, history, rules for memorising— and even of having made himself everything he wore. Sophists like him charged to teach people to speak and to persuade, and travelled from city to city; that is why they spread the idea that truth and laws are <strong>human conventions</strong>, not something fixed by nature. Plato portrayed him with irony, as the sage who knows everything except what matters most.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is knowing about many things the same as being wise? What is the difference between piling up facts and really understanding? You will meet Hippias in Year 2, with the sophists.</p>\n<p>Not everyone trusted that reason. The German <strong><a href=\"#biografias/hamann\">Johann Georg Hamann</a></strong> (1730-1788), a critic of the Enlightenment, held that reason is never <strong>pure</strong>: it is always tied to the <strong>language</strong>, history and faith of each person. He was among the first to recall that, before thinking, we already speak a language that shapes our ideas.</p>\n<h3>Nine attitudes towards knowledge: a path</h3>\n<p>Kant arranged the positions as stages; <a href=\"#biografias/hegel\">Hegel</a> took the idea further. In the <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em> (1807) he goes through the attitudes of consciousness towards truth: each one works for a while, runs into its own contradictions and gives way to another that keeps what was true in it (this is <em>Aufhebung</em>, ‘sublation’). That is why, he says, we should not begin by distrusting everything before knowing: this fear of error turns out, rather, to be fear of the truth. In a classroom version, in the way people talk in class:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Sense-certainty</strong>: ‘what I see is what there is’; it confuses what appears with what is.</li><li><strong>Perception</strong>: it already orders what it sees, but takes its point of view for everyone’s: ‘I don’t get it because the teacher explains it badly’.</li><li><strong>Understanding</strong>: it looks for general laws, like science, but swallows the nuances: ‘if the price goes up, demand goes down; full stop’.</li><li><strong>Stoicism</strong>: ‘nothing affects me, so I can’t go wrong’; it withdraws from the world: ‘I don’t care how it ends’.</li><li><strong>Scepticism</strong>: it demands reasons, but only from others: ‘you haven’t convinced me a hundred per cent, so it isn’t true’.</li><li><strong>Unhappy consciousness</strong>: ‘the truth is up there and I can’t reach it’.</li><li><strong>Moralism</strong>: ‘I’m right because my intentions are good’, or ‘I didn’t do the work, but I tried’.</li><li><strong>Active reason</strong>: ‘we have to act now’, and it gets frustrated when reality won’t fit its scheme.</li><li><strong>Recognition</strong>: ‘truth is built together’, or ‘I don’t understand this; how do you all see it?’.</li></ol>\n<p>Against Hegel’s final reconciliation, <a href=\"#biografias/nietzsche\">Nietzsche</a> proposed leaving the path open. In his notebooks of 1886-1887 he wrote that there are no facts, only interpretations, and in <em>On the Genealogy of Morality</em> (1887, III 12) he specified that there is only a <strong>perspectival</strong> seeing and a perspectival ‘knowing’: the more different eyes we bring to bear on one and the same thing, the more complete our concept of it, our ‘objectivity’, will be. He does not say that anything goes. This is <strong>perspectivism</strong>; in section 8 you will see Ortega’s version, which is not the same.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: in the last argument in class or in a WhatsApp group where you could not agree, which of the nine attitudes were you in? Using the sceptics’ modes, would you have suspended judgement or looked for more reasons?</p>\n<h3>Dictionary: scepticism and attitudes towards knowledge</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pyrrhonism</td><td>The scepticism of Pyrrho and Sextus Empiricus: it neither asserts nor denies that knowledge is possible; it keeps searching and suspends judgement.</td></tr><tr><td><em>Isosthéneia</em></td><td>Equal force of two opposed arguments, which makes it impossible to decide between them.</td></tr><tr><td>Modes (<em>trópoi</em>)</td><td>The sceptics’ standard arguments for opposing appearances or reasons: the ten of Aenesidemus and the five of Agrippa.</td></tr><tr><td><em>Si fallor, sum</em></td><td>‘If I am mistaken, I exist’: Augustine’s answer to scepticism; whoever is mistaken cannot doubt that they exist.</td></tr><tr><td>Perspectivism</td><td>All knowing takes place from a point of view; for Nietzsche, objectivity means adding perspectives together.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3>Describing and understanding: phenomenology and hermeneutics</h3>\n<p>Around 1900 two ways of thinking about knowledge matured that do not quite fit into the table in section 2. The first is the <strong>phenomenology</strong> of <strong><a href=\"#biografias/husserl\">Edmund Husserl</a></strong>, who already appeared in the topic on the human being (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a>). As a theory of knowledge, it proposes not to begin by arguing about whether our ideas copy the world (the dispute between realism and idealism in section 1), but to <strong>describe</strong> how each thing is given to us. A table is never seen whole: it is seen from one side, from a certain height, in a certain light; and yet we perceive ‘a table’, because consciousness fills in what it does not see with what it expects to find if it went round it. To describe this, Husserl asks for an <em>epoché</em>: putting in brackets the belief that the table exists out there, not in order to doubt as the sceptics do, but in order to attend only to how it appears.</p>\n<p>The second is <strong>hermeneutics</strong>, the art of interpreting (from the Greek <em>hermeneúein</em>, ‘to interpret’, ‘to translate’). It arose in order to read difficult texts well — the Bible, laws, the classics —, and <strong><a href=\"#biografias/schleiermacher\">Friedrich Schleiermacher</a></strong> turned it, at the beginning of the 19th century, into a general theory of understanding. <strong>Wilhelm Dilthey</strong> (1833-1911) took the decisive step for the theory of knowledge: he argued that the <strong>human sciences</strong> (history, law, philology; what we now call the humanities and social sciences) have a method of their own. The natural sciences <strong>explain</strong>: they look for the cause of a phenomenon and place it under a general law. The human sciences <strong>understand</strong>: they grasp the meaning of an action or a work from what it expresses, putting themselves in some way in the place of the person who lived it. That Iker’s face goes red is <em>explained</em> by the dilation of his blood vessels; that he blushes just as the teacher reads his composition aloud is <em>understood</em> if we know what that text means to him.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Nature we explain; the life of the mind we understand. <span class=\"per\">— Wilhelm Dilthey, <em>Ideas Concerning a Descriptive and Analytic Psychology</em> (1894)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>Understanding has a curious shape, the <strong>hermeneutic circle</strong>: we understand each part from the whole, and the whole from its parts. An ‘ok.’ with a full stop in a chat can mean agreement or annoyance, and you only know which by reading the whole conversation, which in turn you understand message by message. It is not a vicious circle, but a spiral: each turn corrects the one before. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/gadamer\">Hans-Georg Gadamer</a></strong> (<em>Truth and Method</em>, 1960), a disciple of <a href=\"#biografias/heidegger\">Heidegger</a>, added that we never read from scratch: we bring <strong>prejudices</strong>, in the literal sense of prior judgements, which are not just an obstacle but the condition for understanding anything. Each of us looks from a <strong>horizon</strong>, what we manage to see from our own time and language. Understanding a text from another time is neither abandoning our own horizon to get into the author’s (that would be impossible) nor reading it as if it had been written yesterday, but a <strong>fusion of horizons</strong>. If you read the letters that a great-grandfather who was a shepherd sent from Nevada in the 1920s, you understand words and worries that are no longer yours, and at the same time you discover something about your own family that you did not see before.</p>\n<p>What does this have to do with truth? For hermeneutics there is no view from nowhere: all human knowledge is situated <strong>interpretation</strong>, and that does not make it arbitrary, because the text, or reality, resists and corrects our readings. It is an <strong>open debate</strong>. <strong>Habermas</strong> reproached Gadamer for trusting tradition too much, since it also passes on domination and deceptions (hence his emancipatory interest, which you will see in ‘Knowledge, power and interest’); and <strong>E. D. Hirsch</strong> (<em>Validity in Interpretation</em>, 1967) argued that a text means what its author meant to say, so that not all readings are equally valid. In <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t7\">Topic 7</a> (aesthetics) Gadamer reappears; in Year 2, this discussion continues in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-sospecha\">‘The philosophers of suspicion’</a> and in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-posmodernidad\">‘Nietzsche and postmodernity’</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can the physics of sound waves explain why a song moves you? Would anything be left out? And when you read an old text, who puts in more of the meaning, the author or you?</p>\n<h3>Dictionary: phenomenology and hermeneutics</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Phenomenology</td><td>Husserl’s method: describing how things are given to us in experience before building theories about them.</td></tr><tr><td>Phenomenological <em>epoché</em></td><td>Putting in brackets the belief in the external existence of something in order to attend only to how it appears.</td></tr><tr><td>Hermeneutics</td><td>The art and theory of interpretation; since Dilthey, also the method of the human sciences.</td></tr><tr><td>Explaining / understanding</td><td>Explaining is giving the cause of a fact according to a law; understanding is grasping the meaning of an action or a work.</td></tr><tr><td>Hermeneutic circle</td><td>The parts are understood from the whole and the whole from the parts, in successive turns.</td></tr><tr><td>Fusion of horizons</td><td>For Gadamer, understanding is an encounter between the horizon of the text and that of the reader, which changes both.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>4. The question of truth</h2>\n<p>We have talked a great deal about truth; now it is worth asking what it is. The word has several senses depending on the context: in the <strong>moral</strong> sense, being truthful is saying what one thinks (the opposite of lying); in the <strong>ontological</strong> sense, what is true is what really is; but the sense that interests us here is the <strong>gnoseological</strong> one, in which truth (and its opposite, falsehood) is a property of our <strong>judgements</strong>: a judgement is true when what it says agrees with reality.</p>\n<p>Before truth was a property of judgements, the Greeks thought about it differently. The word was <em>alétheia</em>: the prefix <em>a-</em> negates and <em>léthe</em> means 'oblivion' or 'concealment', so that what is true would be what is <strong>not hidden</strong>, what comes to light. In his poem, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/parmenides\">Parmenides</a></strong> (5th century BC) relates that a goddess shows him two paths: that of <em>alétheia</em>, which only reason travels, and that of the opinions of mortals, in which 'there is no true trust'. Twenty-five centuries later, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/heidegger\">Martin Heidegger</a></strong> (<em>Being and Time</em>, 1927, § 44) returned to that word to argue that the correspondence between judgement and thing is not primary: for a judgement to be able to conform to something, that something must have shown itself beforehand. He called this showing <strong>unconcealment</strong>. Philologists dispute the etymology, and Heidegger himself, in 'The End of Philosophy and the Task of Thinking' (1964), withdrew his thesis that in Greece truth had passed from <em>alétheia</em> (unconcealment) to the correctness of the statement: he acknowledged that that story about the word did not hold up. Even so, the idea recalls something true: before affirming, one must have seen.</p>\n<p>Moreover, in ordinary life we call things, not only sentences, 'true'. A 'true friend', 'real gold' or an 'authentic txakoli' are not judgements that correspond to something: they are things that <strong>answer to what they claim to be</strong>, just as the house of Thomas Aquinas answers to the architect's plan (you will see this a little further down). This is the <strong>truth of things</strong> or authenticity, which the tradition called ontological truth. It is advisable not to mix the levels: 'this txakoli is from Getaria' is a true or false sentence (truth of judgement); 'this txakoli is authentic' says that the thing is not an imitation (truth of the thing); and 'I swear it is from Getaria' commits the person who says it (truthfulness, which you will see in the block on lying). One can even say something true about a false thing: 'this bottle is an imitation'.</p>\n<p><strong>Plato</strong> had already distinguished two degrees of knowledge that remain very useful. On the one hand, <strong>opinion</strong> (<em>doxa</em>): superficial, changing and uncertain knowledge, which comes from the senses and stays with the appearances of things. On the other, <strong>science</strong> or true knowledge (<em>epistêmê</em>): stable, universal and necessary knowledge, which reason attains by grasping the deepest realities (what Plato called the Forms). Believing is not the same as knowing: <em>doxa</em> says “it seems to me”; <em>epistêmê</em> says “I know, and I know why”.</p>\n<p><strong>From the shadows to the Form of the Good.</strong> In the <em>Republic</em> (Books VI and VII), Plato draws a line divided into four steps. The first two are <em>doxa</em>: imagination (<em>eikasía</em>), which remains in shadows and reflections, and belief (<em>pístis</em>), which trusts the things we see and touch. The last two are <em>episteme</em>: mathematical thought (<em>diánoia</em>), which reasons from assumptions and makes use of figures, and intelligence (<em>nóesis</em>), which no longer needs images. The way up is <strong>dialectic</strong>: asking and answering, putting every definition to the test and not stopping until one can give an account of it. At the top is the <strong>Form of the Good</strong>, which Plato compares to the sun: just as the sun makes things visible and gives them life, the Good makes the Forms intelligible and gives them their being. That is why, in the city he imagines, those who have made that journey, the philosophers, should rule (you will see this in Topic 6). In Year 2 you will study it in depth in the topic on <a href=\"#teoria/hf-platon\">Plato</a>, with the allegory of the cave, which is a PAU text.</p>\n<h3>From ‘it occurs to me’ to ‘I know’</h3>\n<p>Between <em>doxa</em> and <em>episteme</em> there is room for more steps. <strong>Kant</strong> set them in order in the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781, in the section ‘On opining, knowing and believing’) with two questions: am I convinced (<strong>subjective</strong> certainty)? Do I have reasons that hold for anyone (<strong>objective</strong> certainty)? If both are missing, I <strong>hold an opinion</strong>; if I am convinced without reasons sufficient for others, I <strong>believe</strong>; if I have both, I <strong>know</strong>. Below these we can put the <strong>passing thought</strong>: what crosses our mind without our yet standing by it. Maialen leaves home in Vitoria-Gasteiz and thinks ‘what if there’s a tram strike today?’ (passing thought); a classmate remarks that there probably is, and it seems possible to her (opinion); her aunt, who always knows what is going on, assures her of it, and Maialen no longer doubts it, although she could not prove it (belief); finally she reads the company’s official notice (knowledge).</p>\n<h3>Cosmology, cosmogony and worldview</h3><p>The three words share a root, the Greek <em>kósmos</em> (order, ordered world), but they do not mean the same, and it is best not to confuse them.</p><ul><li><strong>Cosmology</strong>: the rational study of the order and laws of the universe. The term was spread by Christian Wolff in his <em>Cosmologia generalis</em> (1731), and today it also designates a branch of physics.</li><li><strong>Cosmogony</strong>: the mythical and religious accounts of the origin of the world. Each culture has its own: Hesiod’s <em>Theogony</em> tells how Earth and the generations of gods arose from Chaos; Genesis tells how God created the world in six days.</li><li><strong>Worldview</strong>: a coherent interpretation of the world as a whole, which not only explains but also guides life. A medieval peasant saw a closed, created and hierarchical universe, where each thing had its place and its end; a modern person sees an immense universe, governed by impersonal laws, in which each individual must find their place. The two visions change what is expected of life, not only what is known about the sky.</li></ul><p>It is often said that there was a <strong>move from myth to <em>Lógos</em></strong>: from explaining the world through stories of gods to explaining it through reasons that anyone can discuss. But it was not an absolute break. The first philosophers inherited from myth the question of the origin and the idea of an order; and Plato himself continued to tell myths when argument fell short. Nor has myth disappeared today: it coexists with science in our worldviews.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what worldview lies behind your ideas about the world, even if you have never chosen it? From where have you received it?</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Degree</th><th>Am I convinced?</th><th>Do I have reasons valid for anyone?</th><th>What it sounds like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Passing thought</strong></td><td>Not yet</td><td>No</td><td>‘What if…?’</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Opinion</strong></td><td>Halfway</td><td>No</td><td>‘I think that…’</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Belief</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>No, or they are not enough</td><td>‘I’m sure’</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>‘I know, and I can show why’</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>There is another scale that looks not at what we know, but at what we <strong>say</strong>. As we have just seen, in the moral sense truth is truthfulness, and it is worth separating two relations: that of my words to what I <strong>believe</strong> and that of my words to what <strong>is</strong>. <strong>Lying</strong> is not saying something false, but asserting the opposite of what one thinks with the intention of deceiving: that is how <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> defined it in <em>On Lying</em> (c. 395). That is why you can lie by saying something true. Jon swears to his mother that the Chemistry exam is on Friday, convinced that it is on Thursday, to win a day on his games console; it turns out that it had been moved to Friday. He has <strong>got it right without knowing it</strong>, but he has lied. And the other way round: whoever says what they believe is <strong>sincere</strong>, even if they are wrong. <strong>Bernard Williams</strong> (<em>Truth and Truthfulness</em>, 2002) added that full truthfulness requires two virtues: <strong>sincerity</strong> (saying what you believe) and <strong>accuracy</strong> (taking the trouble to find out before you believe). Someone who has both is <strong>truthful</strong>: ‘I forwarded it in good faith’ is not enough if you did not check it.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Do they say what they believe?</th><th>Is what they say true?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lies</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lies, but gets it right without knowing</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sincere, but mistaken</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Truthful</strong> (sincere and accurate)</td><td>Yes, and has checked it</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if you share a hoax believing it is true, have you lied? Are you responsible for anything? And what about someone who says something true in order to deceive, like ‘I didn’t touch your phone this afternoon’ when they touched it in the morning?</p>\n<p>In the <em>Theaetetus</em>, Plato also examined, without endorsing it (the dialogue ends without an answer), a definition of <strong>knowledge</strong> that remains the starting point of the debate: to know is to have a <strong>true and justified belief</strong>. It is not enough to believe something that happens to be true by chance (someone who guesses the lottery number did not “know” it): good reasons are needed. However, <strong>Edmund Gettier</strong> showed in 1963, with counterexamples, that not even that is enough: one can have a true and justified belief by luck, and that is not knowledge. For example, you look at a clock that has always worked well and believe it is two o’clock; it is, but the clock has stopped and is right by pure luck (the example is Russell’s). What needs to be added to the definition remains an open problem. Moreover, justifying is not easy, as the sceptic <strong>Agrippa</strong> showed with his <strong>trilemma</strong>: every justification needs another to support it, and then either we go on without end (<strong>infinite regress</strong>), or we return to the starting point (<strong>circle</strong>), or we stop at something we do not justify (<strong>dogma</strong>).</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-39\">But what does it mean for a judgement to be true? Throughout history several <strong>theories of truth</strong> have been proposed. The oldest and most intuitive is that of <strong>correspondence</strong> (or adequation): a statement is true if it corresponds to the facts, if it “fits” reality; <strong>Aristotle</strong> had already formulated it precisely.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>To say of what is that it is not, or of what is not that it is, is false, while to say of what is that it is, and of what is not that it is not, is true. — Aristotle, <em>Metaphysics</em></p> </blockquote>\n<p>In the Middle Ages, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/tomas\">Thomas Aquinas</a></strong> (1225-1274) took up the definition that would become classic: truth is the <strong>adequation</strong> of thing and intellect (<em>adaequatio rei et intellectus</em>, in the <em>Disputed Questions on Truth</em>, question 1). But he added a second level. Our intellect is true when it conforms to things; things, in turn, are ‘true’ when they conform to the idea of them held by the <strong>divine intellect</strong>, which created them, just as a house is as it should be when it matches the plan of whoever designed it (the example is Thomas’s own). Each thing thus stands between two intellects: God’s measures it, and it measures ours. For Thomas, human truth is possible because the world was thought before it was known. You will see this in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-fe-razon\">‘Faith and reason’</a>.</p>\n<p>In the 17th century, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/spinoza\">Spinoza</a></strong> (1632-1677) asked a prior question: how do I know that my idea matches the thing, if to compare them I would have to step outside my ideas? He did not deny that a true idea agrees with its object (<em>Ethics</em>, part I, axiom 6), but he replied that truth needs no external judge: whoever has a true idea <strong>knows at the same time that they have it</strong> and cannot doubt it (<em>Ethics</em>, part II, proposition 43). Someone who has understood the proof that there are infinitely many prime numbers does not need anyone to certify that they have understood it; and from that clarity they also see where their earlier mistake went wrong. Hence the image in the scholium to that proposition: just as light reveals both itself and darkness, truth is <strong>the standard both of itself and of the false</strong>. The criterion of truth lies within knowledge, not outside it; that is why Spinoza is usually placed close to the coherence theory, which comes next. You have his system in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">‘Spinoza: philosophy as a system’</a>.</p>\n<p>Alongside it, the theory of <strong>coherence</strong>, characteristic above all of the formal sciences, holds that a statement is true if it contradicts neither itself nor the system of which it is a part. The theory of <strong>consensus</strong> (defended in the 20th century by Habermas) adds that an assertion is true when all those who discuss it rationally would end up accepting it. Not just any consensus will do: it is the one that would be reached in an <strong>ideal speech situation</strong>, with all participants on an equal footing and without coercion or deceit. It is an idealisation, not the result of a vote. And the <strong>pragmatic</strong> theory (the pragmatists, such as William James) measures truth by its <strong>consequences</strong>: what is true is what works and proves useful in practice.</p>\n<table data-bloque=\"fil-t3-42\">\n<thead>\n<tr> <th>Theory</th> <th>What does truth consist in?</th> <th>Example</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td><strong>Correspondence</strong></td> <td>In the judgement agreeing with the facts</td> <td>“It is raining” is true if it is, in fact, raining</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Coherence</strong></td> <td>In the judgement not contradicting the system</td> <td>A theorem is true if it fits without contradiction into the mathematical system</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Consensus</strong></td> <td>In the agreement reached through rational dialogue</td> <td>A norm is accepted as valid after a free debate among everyone</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Pragmatic</strong></td> <td>In the idea working and being useful in practice</td> <td>A medical hypothesis is “true” if its predictions cure</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p>The pragmatic theory deserves a little more detail, because James was not its only defender. It starts with <strong>Charles S. Peirce</strong> (1839-1914), who in ‘How to Make Our Ideas Clear’ (1878) proposed the <strong>pragmatic maxim</strong>: to know what an idea means, think about what conceivable practical effects its being true would have; those effects are all that the idea means. Calling steel ‘hard’ means, for example, that you will not scratch it with your fingernail or with a key. Truth, for Peirce, is the opinion on which everyone who investigated a matter for long enough would end up agreeing (an idea close to that of consensus). <strong><a href=\"#biografias/james\">William James</a></strong> turned it into a theory of truth as what ‘works’ in our experience (<em>Pragmatism</em>, 1907), and <strong><a href=\"#biografias/dewey\">John Dewey</a></strong> applied it to inquiry: an idea is an <strong>instrument</strong>, a hypothesis that is put to the test in solving a problem; that is why he preferred to speak of <strong>warranted assertibility</strong> (<em>Logic: The Theory of Inquiry</em>, 1938) rather than simply of truth.</p>\n<p>Each theory has its classic objection, and knowing them helps us not to take any of them as final. Against the <strong>pragmatic</strong> theory, <strong>Bertrand Russell</strong> (1908) objected that it is easier to know whether something is true than to know whether believing it has good consequences: to know whether Columbus crossed the Atlantic in 1492 there is no need to work out whom it suits to believe it. Besides, there are beliefs that work and are false: ‘tomorrow’s exam will be easy’ is reassuring the night before, and that does not make it true. Against <strong>coherence</strong>, Russell himself (<em>The Problems of Philosophy</em>, 1912) pointed out that there may be several coherent systems that are incompatible with one another: a good novel, or a tightly woven conspiracy theory, do not contradict themselves and are not true. Against <strong>correspondence</strong>, people ask what ‘fact’ ‘2 + 2 = 4’ or ‘torturing for fun is wrong’ corresponds to, and how we could step outside our beliefs to compare them with the world. And against <strong>consensus</strong>, that the ideal speech situation is never fully achieved: how can we know whether a real agreement resembles it closely enough?</p>\n<h3>How do I know that something is true? The criteria of truth</h3>\n<p>The previous theories answer the question 'what is truth?'. But in life we need to answer another: 'how do I know that this is true?'. The first asks for a <strong>definition</strong>; the second, for a <strong>criterion</strong>, a sign or a proof that allows us to recognise what is true. Knowing what fever is is not the same as having a thermometer. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/kant\">Kant</a></strong> saw this clearly in the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781, introduction to 'Transcendental Logic', III): he accepts the definition of truth as the agreement of knowledge with its object, but maintains that there cannot be a <strong>universal</strong> criterion that serves for any content, because it would have to do without precisely the content, which is what distinguishes one object from another. To ask for it, he says with humour quoting the ancients, is like one person milking a billy-goat while another holds a sieve underneath. The only thing that logic offers for all cases is a negative condition: what contradicts itself is false, but what does not contradict itself may still be so. Therefore, in practice, we use several partial criteria. The most common are five; none is infallible and each has its limit:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Authority.</strong> We accept something because it is said by someone who knows: the doctor, the Euskaltzaindia dictionary, the Euskalmet forecast. It is inevitable, because no one can check everything for themselves. But authority is valid <strong>in its field</strong> and for its reasons, not for its prestige: according to Cicero, the disciples of Pythagoras settled discussions with a 'he himself said it' (<em>ipse dixit</em>), and there were professors who refused to look through Galileo's telescope because Aristotle had already spoken. Limit: dogmatism and the fallacy of authority, which cites a famous person outside their field.</li><li><strong>Tradition.</strong> Something is held to be true because it has been transmitted from generation to generation. It preserves accumulated experience: willow bark was used against fever and pain long before chemistry isolated salicylic acid, from which aspirin came. But the antiquity of a belief does not prove it: that one must wait two hours after eating to bathe was repeated for decades without solid evidence. Limit: inertia, which confuses 'it has always been said' with 'it is so'.</li><li><strong>Evidence.</strong> We accept what presents itself with immediate clarity, without need of proof: that the whole is greater than the part, or that I am reading now. <a href=\"#biografias/descartes\">Descartes</a> made it his first rule: to admit nothing as true that does not present itself to the mind so clearly and distinctly that there is no occasion to doubt (<em>Discourse on Method</em>, 1637, II). Limit: what is evident to one is not so to another, and what seemed evident may fall; for centuries it was evident that the Earth is still, because we do not notice it moving.</li><li><strong>Coherence.</strong> A statement gains credit if it fits with what we already know, and loses it if it clashes with it. It is the criterion of the detective and the judge: the witness who says they saw the scene in the full light of the street lamps, when that night there was a power cut, contradicts themselves with an already proven fact. It is excellent for <strong>ruling out</strong>. Limit: an account can be coherent from beginning to end and false, as you have already seen.</li><li><strong>Consent.</strong> We hold as true what the community that knows about the matter accepts; today, for example, that the climate is warming due to human action, on which the immense majority of those who study it agree. It carries great weight when those who agree are competent and independent of one another. Limit: the majority also errs; the doctors of Vienna rejected <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3-semmelweis\">Semmelweis</a> when he asked them to wash their hands.</li></ul>\n<p>None is sufficient alone, and the five correct each other: authority is checked with evidence, tradition with coherence, consent with new evidence. Science adds <strong>public verification</strong>: that anyone, with the appropriate means, can repeat the observation or the experiment. And the pragmatists would add <strong>utility</strong>: that an idea works is a good clue, although, as Russell objected, it does not make it true. Note that authority and tradition rest on the testimony of others: most of what we know we have never checked. That is not a failure of our intelligence, but a condition of life in common; the problem begins when we do not know whom to trust.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Question it asks</th><th>Works well when…</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Authority</strong></td><td>Who says it?</td><td>An expert in their field speaks, without hidden interests</td><td>Dogmatism; the famous person outside their field</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tradition</strong></td><td>Since when is it believed?</td><td>It sums up an experience tested many times</td><td>Inertia and prejudice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Evidence</strong></td><td>Do I see it clearly?</td><td>It is something simple and within everyone's reach</td><td>What is evident to me may be an error</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coherence</strong></td><td>Does it fit with the rest?</td><td>Versions need to be ruled out</td><td>A coherent account can be false</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consent</strong></td><td>Do those who know agree?</td><td>Those who agree are competent and independent</td><td>The majority also errs</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A case for class. At a family meal someone makes two claims: ‘Basque is the oldest language in Europe’ and ‘among the Basques there is more Rh negative than almost anywhere else’. Let us apply the criteria. The first rests mainly on <strong>tradition</strong> and a diffuse <strong>authority</strong> (‘the experts say so’), but it fails on <strong>coherence</strong>: all languages spoken today come, without a break, from earlier ones, so none is ‘older’ in the strict sense. What the <strong>consent</strong> of linguists does support is something else: Basque is the only language predating the arrival of the Indo-European languages that survives in Western Europe. The second sounds like a family legend, but here the <strong>evidence</strong> is not that of the eyes, but that of blood tests: population genetics studies agree that the proportion of Rh negative in the Basque population is among the highest known. Moral: what decides is not how flattering a sentence sounds, but what kind of claim it is and what proof it admits.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: how do you know that the Earth is round? Review the criterion by which you accept it. Have you ever checked it yourself, or does it rest on authority and consent? If so, do you know it any less for that?</p>\n<p>The pragmatic theory had a radical heir in the 20th century: <strong><a href=\"#biografias/rorty\">Richard Rorty</a></strong> (1931-2007). For Rorty, seeking a truth understood as a “mirror of nature” — as if language could copy the world just as it is — is a dead end. What we call “true” would rather be whatever, within a community and a vocabulary, proves most <strong>useful</strong> and defensible for carrying on the conversation and solving problems. It is not that “anything goes”, but a change of question: instead of “does this correspond to ultimate Reality?”, ask “does this way of speaking serve us, is it better than the others for what matters to us?”.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if we stopped seeing truth as a “mirror” of the world, would we lose something essential, or only an illusion? Can an idea be <strong>useful</strong> and at the same time <strong>false</strong>? It is an <strong>open debate</strong> that you will continue in Year 2; you will meet Rorty among the pragmatists and postmodernity.</p>\n<h3>Do we all make truth together? Social construction and pluralism</h3>\n<p>The sociologists <strong>Peter L. Berger</strong> and <strong>Thomas Luckmann</strong> studied in a 1966 book (<em>The Social Construction of Reality</em>) not the truth of the philosophers, but something more everyday: what in a society ‘everyone knows’ and no one questions. That knowledge forms, according to them, in three moments. People <strong>externalise</strong> habits and ways of doing things; these habits are repeated until they become institutions that seem as solid as a mountain (<strong>objectivation</strong>); and each new generation learns them as ‘normal’ (<strong>internalisation</strong>). That in the Basque Country (Euskadi) people have dinner at around nine, that a group of friends lasts from childhood to retirement or that a twenty-euro note is worth something are social realities: they exist because we all uphold them together, and to someone who comes from elsewhere they seem as arbitrary as theirs seem to us.</p>\n<p>Some took a step further and turned this sociology into a theory of truth: if what passes for knowledge depends on society, perhaps ‘true’ means only ‘accepted by my group’. This is strong <strong>social constructivism</strong>, which Berger and Luckmann did not defend. In its favour: it teaches us to be suspicious of what ‘has always been like that’. But it faces serious objections. <strong>John Searle</strong> (<em>The Construction of Social Reality</em>, 1995) distinguished <strong>institutional facts</strong>, which exist because we accept them (the value of money, a goal going up on the scoreboard), from <strong>brute facts</strong>, which depend on no one: the dinosaurs died out 66 million years ago without any society agreeing to it; and the former rest on the latter (the banknote is, when all is said and done, a piece of paper). <strong>Paul Boghossian</strong> (<em>Fear of Knowledge</em>, 2006) added that total constructivism runs into the same problem as the relativism of Protagoras: if all truth is relative to a group, so is the constructivist thesis, which then ceases to bind anyone who does not share it.</p>\n<p>Another way out, more recent, is <strong>pluralism</strong> about truth: perhaps there is not just one way of being true. <strong>Crispin Wright</strong> (<em>Truth and Objectivity</em>, 1992) proposed that ‘true’ always obeys a few minimal rules (to assert something is to present it as true; whatever can be true has its negation; being justified is not the same as being true), but that what makes a sentence true changes from one field to another: in physics it may be correspondence with the facts; in mathematics, proof; in morality or in what we find funny, a justification that would stand up to any scrutiny. Each classic theory would be right in its own field. The main objection is that of <strong>mixed statements</strong>, raised among others by Christine Tappolet (1997 and 2000): ‘the Guggenheim is in Bilbao and it is a masterpiece’ puts together a fact and an evaluation. If each half is true in its own way, in what way is the whole sentence true? It seems that a common truth is needed, precisely the one that pluralism wanted to avoid.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: write down three things that ‘everyone knows’ where you live. Which of them are institutional facts, which exist because we accept them, and which would be true even if no one accepted them? Can something be socially constructed and, at the same time, true?</p>\n<h3>Reading at several levels: from exegesis to suspicion</h3>\n<p>So far we have asked when a sentence is true. But often the question is another: what <strong>does a text mean</strong>, a myth, a poem? It is a very old question. Already in the sixth century BC, Theagenes of Rhegium read Homer as allegory: the war among the gods would be the struggle of the elements (the dry against the wet, the hot against the cold) or of the virtues against the vices. The Jewish and Christian interpreters of Alexandria did something similar with the Bible: <strong>Philo</strong> (first century) distinguished a literal sense and a hidden one, and <strong>Origen</strong> (third century, <em>On First Principles</em>, IV) compared Scripture to the human being, which has body, soul and spirit: three layers of sense. In the fifth century, <strong>John Cassian</strong> (<em>Conferences</em>, XIV, 8) fixed four and explained them with a single word, ‘Jerusalem’, which can be understood in four ways. This is the <strong>exegesis</strong> of the four senses:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Literal</strong> or historical: the city of the Jews, with its walls and its temple.</li><li><strong>Allegorical</strong>: the Church, the community of believers.</li><li><strong>Moral</strong> (or tropological): the soul of each person, which may be at peace or in ruins.</li><li><strong>Anagogical</strong> (from the Greek <em>anagogé</em>, ‘leading upwards’): the heavenly city, the end towards which everything moves.</li></ul>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p><em>Littera gesta docet, quid credas allegoria, / moralis quid agas, quo tendas anagogia.</em> The letter teaches what happened; allegory, what you are to believe; the moral sense, what you are to do; anagogy, where you are heading. <span class=\"per\">— Augustine of Dacia, <em>Rotulus pugillaris</em> (c. 1260), a couplet for students to memorise (own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>The method had a safety rule. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/tomas\">Thomas Aquinas</a></strong> (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, question 1, article 10) warned that all the senses rest on the literal one and that only from this one can one argue: allegory illuminates, but it does not prove. Four centuries later, <a href=\"#biografias/spinoza\">Spinoza</a> (<em>Theological-Political Treatise</em>, 1670, ch. 7) went further: Scripture must be interpreted as nature is studied, from its own data (the Hebrew language, who wrote each book, when and for whom), without seeking in it what the interpreter already wanted to find. From this is born the modern hermeneutics you saw in section 3 with Schleiermacher and Gadamer. The old scheme, in any case, remains a good exercise. Read at four levels ‘The Cicada and the Ant’, the fable that the Álava-born Félix María de Samaniego put into verse in 1781: an insect sings all summer and goes hungry in winter (literal); two ways of living, that of the one who spends and that of the one who saves (allegorical); one must plan ahead (moral); and, if you like, a question about the end: what does one live for, to accumulate or to sing? (anagogical).</p>\n<p>In 1965, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/ricoeur\">Paul Ricoeur</a></strong> (<em>Freud and Philosophy: An Essay on Interpretation</em>) distinguished two ways of interpreting. One approaches a text or a symbol with trust, to hear what it has to say, as the medieval exegetes did: he called it the ‘recollection’ of meaning, and we may call it <strong>hermeneutics of listening</strong>. The other starts from the opposite: the meaning that appears may be a disguise. Ricoeur spoke of interpreting ‘as an exercise of suspicion’; today it is called <strong>hermeneutics of suspicion</strong>. Its three masters taught us to ask, before an idea that presents itself as true, what lies behind it: a class interest that passes itself off as natural (<a href=\"#biografias/marx\">Marx</a>), a will to power that passes itself off as disinterested truth (<a href=\"#biografias/nietzsche\">Nietzsche</a>) or a desire that consciousness does not recognise (<a href=\"#biografias/freud\">Freud</a>). In Year 2 you will study it in depth in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-sospecha\">the masters of suspicion</a>. Here one warning is enough: suspicion serves to review, not to decide. That an idea is born of an interest or a dream does not make it false: the chemist August Kekulé recounted that the ring structure of benzene came to him drowsy before the fireplace, and it is true by experiments, not by the dream. Confusing the origin of a belief with its truth is the <strong>genetic fallacy</strong>.</p>\n<p>One of those masters wrote at the age of 28 the most cited text on truth as language, <em>On Truth and Lies in a Nonmoral Sense</em> (1873), which he left unpublished. It begins with a fable: in a corner of the universe, some clever animals invented knowing; it was the proudest and most mendacious minute in the history of the world, and then the star cooled and those animals died. Then he explains how a word is born. A nerve stimulus becomes an image (first metaphor), and the image becomes a sound (second metaphor); at each step we leap to a different terrain. The concept ‘leaf’ arises when we equate what is unequal: we forget the differences among thousands of leaves, none identical to another, and we believe that ‘the leaf’ exists. To be truthful, in society, would mean using the customary metaphors. And then he asks:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>What then is truth? A mobile army of metaphors, metonymies and anthropomorphisms; in short, a sum of human relations that poetry and rhetoric have heightened, transferred and adorned, and that, after long use, seem to a people solid, canonical and binding. Truths are illusions of which we have forgotten that they are illusions, worn-out metaphors that no longer impress the senses, coins that have lost their relief and no longer count as coins, but as metal. <span class=\"per\">— Nietzsche, <em>On Truth and Lies in a Nonmoral Sense</em> (1873), § 1 (own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>The thesis is not that everything is a lie, but that truth has a <strong>history</strong>: our words began as images, comparisons and human projections, and ‘truths’ are those images when no one remembers that they were, like the worn coin that is weighed instead of counted. Many words confirm this: ‘comprehend’ comes from the Latin <em>comprehendere</em>, ‘to grasp with the hand’. The objections are serious. If all truth is forgotten metaphor, then so is this thesis, and then it has no more authority than the truths it criticises: it is the problem of <strong>self-refutation</strong> that you already saw with Protagoras. And from the metaphorical origin of a word it does not follow that what we say with it is false: ‘the brain processes information’ is a metaphor taken from computing and may be true. Nietzsche himself left behind this youthful writing and later spoke of perspectives rather than lies. Its echo reaches <a href=\"#teoria/hf-posmodernidad\">postmodernity</a>, which you will study in Year 2.</p>\n<h3>Truth and language: Tarski’s definition</h3>\n<p>At the other extreme, the Polish logician <strong>Alfred Tarski</strong> (1901–1983) wanted a definition of truth precise enough to be used in mathematics. He started from Aristotle’s old idea and turned it into a rule: for any sentence, an acceptable definition of ‘true’ must imply its <strong>T-schema</strong>, for example, <em>‘the snow is white’ is true if and only if the snow is white</em>. It seems a triviality, and it is deliberately so. On the left, in quotation marks, a sentence is <strong>named</strong>; on the right it is <strong>used</strong> to speak about the world. This is clearer with two languages: ‘<em>Elurra zuria da</em>’ is true in Basque if and only if the snow is white. The named sentence belongs to the <strong>object language</strong>, the one that speaks about things; the definition of truth is formulated in a <strong>metalanguage</strong>, the one that speaks about that language.</p>\n<p>Why so much precision? To escape the paradox of the <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/mentiroso\">liar</a>: “this sentence is false”. Tarski showed that a language containing its own predicate “true” and able to refer to its own sentences ends in contradiction. The solution is to separate levels: “true in language L” can only be said from a higher language. Tarski insisted (1944) that his definition is <strong>neutral</strong>: it does not say how to find out whether snow is white, nor does it force one to be a realist or an idealist; it defines the word, it does not provide a criterion. Others went further. Frank Ramsey (1927) had already noticed that “it is true that Caesar was murdered” says the same as “Caesar was murdered”. According to the <strong>deflationary</strong> theory, “true” does not name any deep property: it is a device for assenting or for generalising (“everything the witness said is true”) without repeating it all. Its critics reply that, if truth were such a small thing, it would not be understood why we seek it or why it matters so much to us that we should not be deceived.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose a proverb, for example ‘God helps those who rise early’, and read it at the four levels. Then subject it to suspicion: who stands to gain if we believe it?, does that tell you anything about whether it is true? Finally, write its T-schema: ‘“God helps those who rise early” is true if and only if…’. What would have to happen in the world for it to be true?</p>\n<h3>Dictionary: truth and interpretation</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>Alétheia</em></td><td>Greek word for ‘truth’; according to a debated etymology, ‘the unconcealed’. Heidegger understood it as unconcealment.</td></tr><tr><td>Criterion of truth</td><td>Sign or procedure for recognising whether something is true (authority, tradition, evidence, coherence, consent). It is not the same as the definition of truth.</td></tr><tr><td>Exegesis</td><td>Detailed interpretation of a text, especially a sacred one.</td></tr><tr><td>Anagogical sense</td><td>In medieval exegesis, what a text says about the ultimate destiny; the fourth level of reading.</td></tr><tr><td>Hermeneutics of suspicion</td><td>Interpretation that seeks, beneath the manifest meaning, hidden interests, drives or desires (Marx, Nietzsche and Freud, according to Ricoeur).</td></tr><tr><td>Genetic fallacy</td><td>Judging an idea true or false by its origin rather than by its reasons.</td></tr><tr><td>T-schema</td><td>Tarski’s condition: the sentence ‘p’ is true if and only if p.</td></tr><tr><td>Metalanguage</td><td>Language with which one speaks about another language, the object language.</td></tr><tr><td>Deflationary theory</td><td>The theory that holds that ‘true’ does not name a substantial property, but rather serves to assent and to generalise.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>5. Post-truth and <em>fake news</em></h2>\n<h3>Conviction and persuasion</h3><p>You have already seen how Kant ordered opinion, belief and knowledge. In that same section of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (in the “Canon of Pure Reason”, A820-822/B848-850) he makes another very useful distinction. Taking something to be true can rest on two kinds of ground. There is <strong>conviction</strong> when the ground is <strong>objective</strong>, that is, it holds for any rational being who examines it. There is <strong>persuasion</strong> when the ground lies only in the peculiarity of the subject: in his tastes, fears, desires or way of being. From inside, the two feel the same: in both cases I am sure. That is why Kant says that persuasion is an appearance of truth.</p><p>How then to distinguish them? Kant proposes an external <strong>touchstone</strong>: <strong>communicability</strong>. If what I hold can be communicated with reasons and anyone who thinks carefully could come to accept it, it is probably conviction. If only I hold it, or those who resemble me, it is persuasion. I may keep persuasion to myself, but I must not present it as valid for others.</p><p>Compare two certainties. That the angles of a triangle add up to 180° I can prove to anyone, whatever team they support: it is a conviction. That Athletic “is the best team in the world” I feel with all my soul, but if someone from another city does not see it that way, I have no proof to offer him: it is a persuasion. That is why an advertisement that sells us a perfume with epic music and a famous face, without saying anything about the product, does not seek to convince but to persuade: it acts on what is particular to each person, not on reasons that we could discuss.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: how many of your certainties are convictions and how many persuasions? Choose one and ask yourself whether you could defend it before someone who does not share your tastes or your group.</p>\n<p>Truth is not only a theoretical matter: it is also, increasingly, a <strong>political weapon</strong>. The thinker Hannah Arendt warned that truth tends to come off worst against power, and that nevertheless power never manages to replace it completely: a truth can be destroyed by lies or force, but not replaced by something else that takes its place. Today that old tension has taken new forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Post-truth</strong> is the term used for situations in which, in forming public opinion, <strong>objective facts</strong> weigh less than <strong>emotions</strong> and personal beliefs; the Oxford dictionary chose it as <strong>word of the year</strong> in 2016. In post-truth it matters less whether something is true than whether it “sounds” convincing or confirms what we already wanted to believe. Hence the flourishing of <strong>disinformation</strong> (falsehoods spread knowingly, in order to deceive), <strong>misinformation</strong> (falsehoods spread without knowing they are false; in Spanish, <em>desinformación</em> and <em>información errónea</em>) and <strong>hoaxes</strong> or <em>fake news</em>: invented news that spreads at great speed, above all through social media.</p>\n<p>Why now? For almost the whole of history, the problem was the <strong>scarcity</strong> of information. Around 1800 there were about a billion people in the world and, around 1820, only one in eight could read; a book was an expensive object, copied by hand or printed in short runs. Gutenberg’s <strong>printing press</strong> (around 1450) multiplied texts: before 1501 some thirty thousand editions had already been printed. And even then there were people who complained about the excess: in 1545 the Swiss naturalist Conrad Gessner lamented the ‘confusing and harmful abundance of books’ (although Seneca, in the 1st century, had already warned that many books scatter the attention). Then came the daily press, radio, television and, from 1991, the <strong>web</strong> that Tim Berners-Lee had proposed at CERN. Today there are more than 8 billion of us, almost nine out of ten adults can read and about 5.5 billion people use the internet. Never have so many people been able to read and publish so much, and that is why the problem has been reversed: it is no longer finding information, but <strong>choosing</strong> among too much of it, without forgetting that behind every message there is someone with an intention.</p>\n<p>Several mechanisms explain why we fall for them so easily. Cognitive <strong>biases</strong> —especially <em>confirmation bias</em>— make us seek and accept what reinforces our ideas and reject what contradicts them. <strong>Echo chambers</strong> (and the algorithms’ “filter bubbles”) surround us with messages similar to our own, so that we hear only the echo of our own opinion and believe that “everyone” thinks the same. In this way, a lie repeated a thousand times begins to look like truth.</p>\n<p>What we saw in section 1 helps to understand this: if perception filters according to what we expect and memory remakes our memories, it is not surprising that the news that fits our ideas sticks and the news that contradicts them is forgotten. On top of this comes <strong>information overload</strong> (or <em>infoxication</em>). As early as 1971 the economist Herbert Simon warned that an abundance of information creates a scarcity of <strong>attention</strong>. With hundreds of messages and headlines a day there is no time to check anything: we get tired, we become desensitised and we end up believing whatever arrives first or is repeated most.</p>\n<p>The use of <strong>post-truth</strong> shot up in 2016, with the Brexit referendum and the elections in the United States; hence Oxford’s choice. In January 2017, the White House adviser Kellyanne Conway defended on television a false figure about the crowd at Donald Trump’s inauguration by saying that <strong>‘alternative facts’</strong> had been given; the journalist replied that those were not facts, but falsehoods. The expression is an <strong>oxymoron</strong>: there can be alternative interpretations of a fact, not alternative facts. If each group has ‘its own’ facts, <strong>parallel realities</strong> form and discussion becomes impossible, because the common ground on which to disagree is missing.</p>\n<p>Why would anyone prefer ‘alternative facts’? Often, because of <strong>group identity</strong>. In 1954 Albert Hastorf and Hadley Cantril showed a recording of a very rough American football match between two universities to students from both: each group ‘saw’ far more fouls by the opponents than by their own side. This is <strong>motivated reasoning</strong>: we do not reason like an impartial judge, but like a lawyer who already knows what conclusion he wants. If a belief is part of who we are (our group of friends, the team, the party), changing it feels like betraying our own people. And it is not a matter of one ideology: a large meta-analysis (Ditto and others, 2019) found this bias to a similar degree on the left and on the right.</p>\n<p>And why, and for what purpose, do people deceive? For <strong>power</strong> (winning votes, sinking a rival, covering up a mistake) and for <strong>money</strong>: in 2016 some young people in Veles, in what is now North Macedonia, filled the internet with invented news about the American campaign because every click earned them advertising revenue; they did not care who won. Harry Frankfurt gave a key in <em>On Bullshit</em> (1986; in Spanish, <em>Sobre la manipulación de la verdad</em>): the liar still takes the truth into account, even if only to hide it; to the <strong>bullshitter</strong> it is a matter of <strong>indifference</strong>: he says whatever suits him, whether it is true or false.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Bullshit is a greater enemy of the truth than lies are.</p><span class=\"per\">— Harry G. Frankfurt, <em>On Bullshit</em> (1986)</span></blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"#biografias/arendt\">Hannah Arendt</a>, in ‘Truth and Politics’ (1967), added a disturbing idea: when lying is systematic, the result is not so much that the lie is believed as that we lose the sense that orients us in the real world and stop believing in anything at all. Others qualify the diagnosis: the cognitive scientist Hugo Mercier (<em>Not Born Yesterday</em>, 2020) argues that we are less gullible than is feared, and some warn that the fear of post-truth can serve as an excuse for curbing freedom of expression. The debate is still open.</p>\n<p>Above we named echo chambers and filter bubbles together, but they are not the same thing. We <strong>build</strong> the <strong>echo chamber</strong> ourselves: we choose whom to follow, which groups to be in, whom to mute, and in the end we only hear voices that repeat our own; the philosopher C. Thi Nguyen (2020) adds that inside it we are also taught to <strong>distrust</strong> outsiders. The <strong>filter bubble</strong>, on the other hand, is <strong>put around us</strong>: Eli Pariser (<em>The Filter Bubble</em>, 2011) gave this name to the effect of the <strong>algorithms</strong> that personalise what we see (a social network’s feed, the search engine, the recommended videos) according to what we clicked on before, so as to keep us there and sell advertising. We do not choose it and it is invisible: we do not know what has been left out.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Echo chamber</th><th>Filter bubble</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who puts it up?</td><td>We and our group</td><td>An algorithm</td></tr><tr><td>Can we see it?</td><td>Yes, if we pay attention</td><td>No: we do not know what is left out</td></tr><tr><td>The other voices…</td><td>are excluded and discredited</td><td>simply do not reach us</td></tr><tr><td>How do you get out?</td><td>By listening seriously to people who think differently</td><td>By looking beyond the recommendations and varying your sources</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A case. A derby between Athletic and Real; in the last minute the referee awards a penalty to Real. Ane, from Getxo and an Athletic fan, spends the night watching videos on her phone by red-and-white supporters who ‘prove’, in slow motion, that it was not one: the app has learnt that these videos keep her watching (<strong>filter bubble</strong>). In her friends’ group chat everyone thinks the same, and Unai, the only txuri-urdin, was muted months ago (<strong>echo chamber</strong>). On Monday Ane is sure that ‘everyone’ saw the robbery, and she remembers the play more sharply than she saw it live (<strong>reconstructive memory</strong>). Perhaps she is right; but her reasons for being so sure have little to do with the play.</p>\n<p>Why the mind takes these shortcuts, and which biases are most common, is explained in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t4\">Topic 4</a>, in the section on fallacies.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: open the app you use most. What does it show you and what do you think it is not showing you? When did you last change your mind because of something you read or heard? If it never happens to you, is that because you are always right or because the only people who reach you are the ones who agree with you?</p>\n<h3>Conspiracy theories: suspicion without method</h3>\n<p>A <strong>conspiracy</strong> is a fact: several people secretly agree to achieve something, often illegal, that they could not do in broad daylight. A <strong>conspiracy theory</strong> is something else: a story that explains an important event (a pandemic, an attack, an election) as the hidden, deliberate work of a powerful group that deceives everyone else. In a report by the Catalan fact-checker Verificat (2024), a sociologist points out three recurring features: they <strong>challenge the consensus</strong> (what science, judges or the press say), they point to <strong>elites acting in the shadows</strong> and they live off a <strong>community</strong> that feeds itself. Some last for centuries, such as those that have accused Jews since the Middle Ages; others are born with each new technology, such as the one that linked 5G to Covid-19.</p>\n<p>The problem is that there are also <strong>real conspiracies</strong>, and they are documented. The <strong>Watergate</strong> espionage led to President Nixon’s resignation in 1974. In the <strong>Tuskegee study</strong> (1932-1972), the US Public Health Service left the syphilis of nearly 400 Black men untreated in order to observe the disease, even after penicillin had become the standard treatment in 1947. <strong>Operation Northwoods</strong> (1962) was a plan by the US Joint Chiefs of Staff to stage attacks and justify a war against Cuba; the Kennedy administration rejected it, and it was made public in 1997. The CIA’s <strong>MK-Ultra</strong> programme (1953-1973) gave LSD to people who did not know it. In Spain, 27 deaths are attributed to the <strong>GAL</strong>, para-police groups active between 1983 and 1987; in 1998 the Supreme Court convicted a former Minister of the Interior, a former Secretary of State for Security and other defendants of the unlawful detention of Segundo Marey (1983) and of misappropriation of public funds. By contrast, microchips in vaccines, the fake moon landing of 1969 and ‘chemtrails’ are hoaxes: nothing supports them.</p>\n<p>What separates the one from the other? Not that they sound unbelievable: Northwoods does too. Real conspiracies have <strong>named culprits</strong>, a <strong>limited scope</strong> in time and in the number of people involved, <strong>documents</strong> and, often, <strong>court rulings</strong>. And look at who uncovered them: journalists, parliamentary committees, judges, an official who spoke out; in other words, the very institutions that a conspiracy theory assumes have been bought. Besides, the more people are needed to keep a secret, the sooner it breaks: the physicist David R. Grimes (2016) calculated with a mathematical model that faking the Moon landing would have required the complicity of some 411,000 people (NASA’s workforce at its peak) and that the hoax would have been uncovered in about three years and eight months. His model simplifies a great deal, but the idea holds. The ‘total’ conspiracy theory, by contrast, is global, credits the enemy with almost infinite power and explains everything.</p>\n<p>That last sentence is the logical key. A good theory, as you will see with <a href=\"#biografias/popper\">Popper</a> in section 6, takes a risk: it says which observation would refute it. The total conspiracy theory is <strong>unfalsifiable</strong> because it is <strong>self-sealing</strong>: if there is no evidence, it is because it has been erased very well; if an expert refutes it, the expert has been bought; if counter-evidence appears, it is a set-up. Every refutation becomes a confirmation. Moreover, it reverses the <strong>burden of proof</strong>: whoever asserts something must prove it, but the conspiracist demands that others show there is no plot. This is the fallacy of appeal to ignorance (section 6): the fact that something has not been shown to be false does not make it true.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The conspiracy theory of society holds that explaining a social event consists in discovering who had an interest in its happening and secretly planned it. <span class=\"per\">— Karl Popper, <em>Conjectures and Refutations</em> (1963), ch. 4 (our own paraphrase, abridged)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>Popper does not deny that there are conspiracies: he says they are frequent, but that they <strong>rarely turn out as planned</strong>. Social life is full of <strong>unintended consequences</strong>: nobody decides that rents should rise or that a village should empty, and yet it happens, through the sum of many decisions; studying those effects that nobody wanted is, for him, the main task of the social sciences. Popper also sees the conspiracy theory as a <strong>secularised superstition</strong>: where Homer’s Greeks explained the Trojan War by the intrigues of the gods of Olympus, the conspiracist puts bankers, governments or ‘elites’. The characters change, but the pattern is the same: if something happens, somebody wanted it.</p>\n<p>Why are they so attractive? The psychologist Karen Douglas and her colleagues (2017) speak of three needs. An <strong>epistemic</strong> one: to understand what is going on and to have certainties. An <strong>existential</strong> one: to feel safe and in <strong>control</strong>; that is why, as Jan-Willem van Prooijen and Douglas herself showed, they flourish in times of crisis, fear and uncertainty, such as the 2020 pandemic. And a <strong>social</strong> one: to feel part of a special group, of those who ‘have woken up’, as opposed to the ‘herd’. Add to this the <strong>proportionality bias</strong>: we expect a big event to have a big cause. In an experiment (Leman and Cinnirella, 2007), people who read that a fictional president had died in an attack believed in a plot more than those who read that he had survived the same shot.</p>\n<p>Even so, is it irrational to believe in conspiracies? Philosophers disagree. Brian Keeley (‘Of Conspiracy Theories’, 1999) argues that no criterion allows us to rule them out in advance, but that there are <strong>warning signs</strong>: explaining away any contrary data as deception and needing ever more conspirators, until one distrusts every institution at once. Charles Pigden (1995, 2007) goes further: history is full of plots, and dismissing them all would be another superstition; we must judge <strong>case by case</strong>, according to the evidence. This is <strong>particularism</strong>. Opposite it stands <strong>generalism</strong>: Quassim Cassam (<em>Conspiracy Theories</em>, 2019) distinguishes ordinary conspiracies, which are sometimes proved, from capitalised ‘Conspiracy Theories’, speculative and contrary to expert knowledge, which work as political <strong>propaganda</strong> rather than as hypotheses. Some fear credulity; others, a society that can no longer suspect the powerful.</p>\n<h3>Dictionary: conspiracy theories</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conspiracy theory</td><td>A story that explains a major event as the secret, deliberate work of a powerful group that deceives everyone else.</td></tr><tr><td>Unfalsifiable (self-sealing) theory</td><td>One that turns any counter-evidence into further evidence in its favour, so that nothing could refute it.</td></tr><tr><td>Burden of proof</td><td>The obligation to give reasons, which falls on whoever asserts something, not on whoever doubts it.</td></tr><tr><td>Proportionality bias</td><td>The tendency to assume that a big event must have an equally big cause.</td></tr><tr><td>Unintended consequences</td><td>Effects of the sum of many actions that nobody sought or planned (Popper).</td></tr><tr><td>Particularism / generalism</td><td>Judging each conspiracy theory case by case, on its evidence / holding that, as a class, they deserve distrust from the outset.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: sort these cases into three groups — A) proven real conspiracy, B) plausible but unproven, C) unfalsifiable: ‘social networks tune their algorithms to make us angry, because that way we spend more time on them’; ‘man never went to the Moon’; ‘governments use crises to pass measures we would not accept in normal times’. What criterion do you use: documents, court rulings, how many people would have to keep quiet? And what if the problem is not that we suspect too much, but that we suspect badly?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-49\">Against all this, the best defence is <strong>critical thinking</strong> and <strong>checking sources</strong>. Faced with any piece of information it is worth asking: who is saying it and with what intention? What evidence do they offer? Is it confirmed by independent and reliable sources? Do I distinguish facts from opinions? Checking before sharing, distrusting headlines that only seek to provoke outrage and consulting more than one source are nowadays essential skills for not being manipulated. The Enlightenment tradition also holds that in a democracy, citizens who are able to tell truth from lies are the best guarantee of freedom.</p>\n<p><strong>Is someone who trusts nothing a ‘researcher’?</strong> Many hoaxes present themselves as critical thinking: ‘I do my own research.’ But doubting everything is not the same as thinking for oneself: one must also know whom to believe and why. For that underlying problem, whom to trust when we know almost everything through others, you have the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-experto\">‘The expert and the evidence’</a>.</p>\n<h3>Beyond the lie: hyperreality</h3>\n<p>If post-truth puts emotions ahead of facts, the French philosopher <strong>Jean Baudrillard</strong> went a step further. In <em>Simulacra and Simulation</em> (1981) he argued that the media and screens have so multiplied the images of the real that many of them no longer copy any original: he called that image with no model behind it a <strong>simulacrum</strong>, and the resulting world <strong>hyperreality</strong>, often sharper and more attractive than reality itself —the television live broadcast, the photo filter, the social-media profile—. The danger then is not only that we are lied to about a fact, but that we stop missing the fact at all, content with its representation. It connects with the relativism of Topic 5 and leaves an open debate: do we live among events or among their images?</p>\n<h3>Knowledge, power and interest</h3>\n<p>The best defence against misinformation is cross-checking sources; but there is a prior question: <strong>knowledge is not neutral</strong>. It matters who <strong>funds</strong> a piece of research, who decides <strong>what is investigated</strong> (and what is not), and who benefits from a claim passing as true. This does not mean that truth does not exist, but that knowledge is born amid interests, and it is worth learning to see them.</p>\n<p>The phrase that sums it up is four centuries old: <strong>‘knowledge is power’</strong>. Francis Bacon wrote it in Latin, <em>nam et ipsa scientia potestas est</em> (‘knowledge itself is also power’, <em>Meditationes Sacrae</em>, 1597): whoever knows how nature works can master it. In the twentieth century, <strong>Jürgen Habermas</strong> added that all knowledge answers to an <strong>interest</strong>; in <em>Knowledge and Human Interests</em> (1968) he distinguishes three: the <strong>technical</strong> (knowing in order to control things, characteristic of the empirical sciences), the <strong>practical</strong> (knowing in order to understand one another, characteristic of the historical and interpretive sciences) and the <strong>emancipatory</strong> (knowing in order to free ourselves from dependence and deception, characteristic of the critical theories). <strong><a href=\"#biografias/foucault\">Michel Foucault</a></strong> went further still with his formula <strong>power/knowledge</strong>: each era decides—in schools, prisons, hospitals—what counts as truth and who has the authority to state it, so that power and knowledge sustain each other (<em>Discipline and Punish</em>, 1975).</p>\n<p>A case close to home: over the course of decades, research funded by large <strong>tobacco</strong> companies generated uncertainty about the link between tobacco and cancer, while other studies paid for by the <strong>sugar industry</strong> shifted the focus of cardiac research towards fats. Work of this kind can be formally valid and yet <strong>self-interested</strong>: whoever pays decides which questions are asked. That is why it is not enough to compare sources: we must also ask <strong>who funds it</strong> and <strong>what nobody is researching</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if knowledge is never entirely neutral, should we distrust all science funded by companies, or is it enough to demand that the funding and the results be made public?</p>\n<h3>Ignorance is manufactured too</h3>\n<p>We usually see ignorance as a gap that science gradually fills. <strong>Robert N. Proctor</strong>, who teaches the history of science at Stanford, proposed studying how it is <strong>produced</strong> as well, and he called that study <strong>agnotology</strong> (from the Greek <em>agnōsis</em>, ‘not knowing’). In a collective volume he edited with <strong>Londa Schiebinger</strong> in 2008 he distinguishes three kinds:</p>\n<ul><li>As a <strong>starting point</strong>: what we know we do not know, which drives us to investigate.</li><li>As <strong>lost ground</strong> through a selection: by investigating some things, others are left in the shade. Schiebinger studied how eighteenth-century European naturalists brought the ‘peacock flower’ back from the Americas as an ornamental plant, but not the knowledge of the enslaved women who used it to induce abortions: it was of no interest, and Europe lost it.</li><li>As a <strong>strategy</strong>: doubt sown on purpose. An internal document from a tobacco company put it bluntly in 1969: ‘Doubt is our product’.</li></ul>\n<p>A case different from tobacco: <strong>leaded petrol</strong>. From the 1920s, almost all the petrol in the world contained a lead compound that improved engines. For decades the leading expert on its effects was Robert Kehoe, whose laboratory was paid for by the companies in the industry, and his argument was clever: lead in the blood is ‘normal’ and, as long as nobody proves that those doses cause harm, there is no reason to ban it. What had not been investigated was taken to be harmless (this is the fallacy of ignorance, which you will see in section 6). In 1965 the geochemist <strong>Clair Patterson</strong> showed that we carried in our bodies about a hundred times more lead than ancient humans, and he lost contracts for saying so. Today we know that it damages children’s brains even at low doses. Spain banned that petrol in 2001; the last country that still sold it, in 2021.</p>\n<h3>Case study: forming an opinion in the face of a scientific consensus</h3>\n<h3>The universe as a machine: mechanism and determinism</h3><p>In his <em>Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy</em> (1687), Isaac Newton showed that a few laws explained at once the fall of an apple and the motion of the planets. From that a powerful image was born: the universe as a <strong>mechanism</strong>, matter in motion governed by laws. Pierre-Simon Laplace took it to the extreme in the introduction to his <em>A Philosophical Essay on Probabilities</em> (1814): an intelligence that knew in an instant all the forces of nature and the position of all bodies, and was capable of calculating with those data, would see the future and the past with complete accuracy. That imaginary intelligence was later called the “Laplace’s demon”. This is <strong>determinism</strong>: everything that happens follows necessarily from what preceded it.</p><p>Think of an ideal billiard table, without unforeseen friction: if you know the position of each ball, the force and direction of the stroke, you can calculate the entire shot before playing it. For the determinist, the universe is that table, only immense.</p><p>The problem arises with <strong>mechanistic reductionism</strong>: applying the method of physics to all of reality, including the human being. If everything is mechanism, the question of the meaning of the universe ceases to have a place, and freedom becomes difficult to think: my decision would be just another ball pushed by the previous ones.</p><p>Kant confronted this tension in the third antinomy of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> and in the <em>Critique of Practical Reason</em> (1788). As a phenomenon, the human being is subject to causes like any body; but when they act and ask themselves what they ought to do, they must also think of themselves as a moral agent, capable of starting something by themselves. The moral law, he said, is what makes us know our freedom.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if everything were determined, would it make sense to call someone to account for what they have done? And to congratulate them?</p>\n<p>Unai sees a video on his phone with thousands of ‘likes’: ‘The climate has always changed; CO₂ is food for plants; scientists make a living out of scaring us’. He is not a climatologist and cannot check the data on his own, so the reasonable question is <strong>whom to trust and why</strong>. The Sixth Assessment Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC, 2021), which summarises thousands of studies, concludes that it is <strong>unequivocal</strong> that human activity has warmed the atmosphere, ocean and land; and a review of more than 88,000 papers (Lynas et al., 2021) found that more than 99% accept it. The video has some truth in it, but climate science has already taken it into account: past changes had causes (the Earth’s orbit, large eruptions) that do not explain the present warming. And doubt does not always arise on its own: between 1977 and 2003 scientists at the oil company Exxon calculated accurately how much the planet would warm, while the company funded campaigns that presented it as uncertain (Supran, Rahmstorf and Oreskes, <em>Science</em>, 2023): Proctor’s third kind of ignorance.</p>\n<p>A checklist that also works for other cases (vaccines, GM crops, nuclear power):</p>\n<ul><li><strong>What do the institutions say</strong> that review all the evidence, rather than a single study or someone with a degree?</li><li><strong>Consensus is neither unanimity nor dogma</strong>: it is the agreement of many independent pieces of evidence. What would overturn it is new evidence, not a video.</li><li><strong>Who is funding</strong> the person sowing doubt?</li><li><strong>Beware of false balance</strong>: a talk show with one scientist for and another against suggests a tie that does not exist.</li><li><strong>Separate the scientific question from the political one</strong>. Whether the warming is caused by humans is a question of fact; what to do about it is an <strong>open debate</strong>: William Nordhaus (Nobel Prize in Economics, 2018) argues for a gradual carbon tax; Bjørn Lomborg, for prioritising adaptation and innovation because of their cost; Naomi Klein, for a thorough change of the economic system.</li><li><strong>What would make you change your mind?</strong> If the answer is ‘nothing’, you are no longer thinking: you are defending an identity.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose another topic with a scientific consensus and a lot of noise on social media and run it through the checklist. How much of your opinion rests on evidence, and how much on what your group thinks?</p>\n<h3>System and lifeworld</h3>\n<p>In his major work, <em>The Theory of Communicative Action</em> (1981), <strong><a href=\"#biografias/habermas\">Habermas</a></strong> distinguished two ways of coordinating what we do together. In the <strong>lifeworld</strong> (family, group of friends, class, associations, public debate) we coordinate by <strong>reaching understanding</strong>: we talk, give reasons and come to agreements against a background of meanings, customs and norms that we share without thinking about it. In the <strong>system</strong> —the economy and the state administration— coordination works without any need to agree, through <strong>money</strong> and <strong>power</strong>: you do not argue with the supermarket about the price of bread; you pay it or you do not. The system is essential in complex societies; the danger, according to Habermas, is the <strong>colonisation of the lifeworld</strong>: the logic of money and power invading areas that only work well with reasons.</p>\n<p>Possible examples: studying being reduced to adding up tenths of a point for the university entrance mark and no longer being about learning; a social network measuring friendship in ‘likes’ because that sells advertising; in a health centre, sticking to the minutes allowed per patient counting for more than listening to them. It is an <strong>open debate</strong>. The sociologist Niklas Luhmann, who debated with Habermas from 1971 onwards, described society as a set of systems (economy, law, science, politics), each with its own code, with no common centre of understanding to protect; and some point out that the market and impersonal rules also free us from depending on the favour of those close to us. You will see it in Year 2, in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-capitalismo\">‘The critique of capitalism’</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: think of something you do for pleasure with your group of friends or your family. Would it change if you started charging for it or giving it marks? For better or for worse?</p>\n\n<p>That distrust of the “grand narratives” that claimed to explain everything has its own name in philosophy: <strong>postmodernity</strong>. In <em>The Postmodern Condition</em> (1979), <strong><a href=\"#biografias/lyotard\">Jean-François Lyotard</a></strong> (1924-1998) defined it as the end of <strong>metanarratives</strong>: the great stories (progress, liberating reason, emancipation) by which an age justified its knowledge and its norms. There is no longer a single story that gives meaning to everything; many language games remain, local and plural.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Simplifying to the extreme, we define “postmodern” as incredulity toward metanarratives. — Jean-François Lyotard, <em>The Postmodern Condition</em> (1979), introduction</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Along the same line, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/derrida\">Jacques Derrida</a></strong> (1930-2004) taught how to <strong>deconstruct</strong>: to read texts — and also ideas, laws or institutions — looking for their internal tensions, what they take for granted and what they leave out. For Derrida, no text has a single fixed, closed meaning; meaning is never fully present, it always shifts. It is not about destroying, but about showing that things could be read — and organised — otherwise.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if several readings of the same text (a law, a news item, an advert) are possible, who decides which one holds? Does that free us or leave us without firm ground? It is an <strong>open debate</strong> that you will continue in Year 2; you can place Lyotard and Derrida in the <a href=\"#genealogias/sos\">“Suspicion and postmodernity”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<p>The Italian <strong><a href=\"#biografias/vattimo\">Gianni Vattimo</a></strong> (1936-2023) went a step further with what he called <strong>weak thought</strong> (<em>pensiero debole</em>): once the great absolute foundations have fallen, truth no longer imposes itself like a rock, but becomes <strong>interpretation</strong>, historical and plural, always open to dialogue. However, this should not be confused with post-truth: weak thought calls for <strong>humility</strong> before truth (recognising that our certainties are interpretable and improvable), not for <strong>lying</strong> knowingly. To doubt that a single, definitive Truth exists is not the same as accepting any old hoax.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can one hold that there is no absolute truth and, at the same time, go on telling the true from the false in daily life? Where is the border between humility before truth and “it’s all the same”? You will study Vattimo in Year 2; you can place him in the <a href=\"#genealogias/sos\">“Suspicion and postmodernity”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<h2>6. Scientific knowledge</h2>\n<p>Of all the kinds of knowledge, one has acquired a special prestige: <strong>scientific</strong> knowledge. <strong>Science</strong> is a system of rational knowledge that draws conclusions from data or premises. The <strong>empirical sciences</strong> explain facts of nature and society on the basis of data; the <strong>formal sciences</strong>, such as logic and mathematics, prove their statements from axioms. Its characteristic features are easy to recognise: it is <strong>rational</strong> (guided by logic), <strong>objective</strong> (it seeks to fit reality), <strong>systematic</strong> (it arranges its knowledge in coherent theories), <strong>methodical</strong> (it follows rigorous procedures) and <strong>verifiable</strong> (its claims can be tested).</p>\n<p>Not all sciences are the same. The <strong>formal sciences</strong>, such as <strong>logic</strong> and <strong>mathematics</strong>, do not study facts about the world, but abstract forms and relations; their statements are proved by pure logical coherence, without the need for experiments. The <strong>empirical sciences</strong>, by contrast, study reality and need to test their claims against experience; they are divided into <strong>natural sciences</strong> (such as physics, chemistry or biology) and <strong>social sciences</strong> or human sciences (such as history, economics or sociology).</p>\n<p>The Argentine <strong>Mario Bunge</strong> (1919-2020) called the empirical sciences <strong>factual sciences</strong>, because they deal with facts (in Latin, <em>facta</em>). A proven theorem is not revised; a law of biology, however well confirmed, remains open to a new piece of data. The social sciences also study people who give meaning to what they do, and so they combine approaches. Why do many young people in the Basque Country who know Basque speak Spanish with their friends? A survey gives percentages (<strong>quantitative</strong> approach); interviews, each person’s reasons (<strong>qualitative</strong>); grasping what switching language means to them is to <strong>interpret</strong>, Dilthey’s ‘understanding’; and looking for the pressures and power relations that encourage it is the <strong>critical</strong> approach.</p>\n<p>How does science work? Through <strong>method</strong>. The <strong>inductive method</strong> starts from the observation of many particular cases to arrive at a general law (if I observe that many ravens are black, I generalise that “all ravens are black”); it has the drawback that we can never observe <em>all</em> cases, so it reaches only probable conclusions. That is why the empirical sciences use above all the <strong>hypothetico-deductive method</strong>, systematised from <strong>Galileo</strong> onwards: faced with a problem a <strong>hypothesis</strong> is formulated, consequences are deduced from it and then tested experimentally; if they are confirmed, the hypothesis becomes a law; if not, another is sought.</p>\n<p>A complete case of this method, with one hypothesis refuted and another confirmed, is <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3-semmelweis\">‘The hypothetico-deductive method: the Semmelweis case’</a>; the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-ciencia\">‘What makes a theory scientific?’</a> links it to Popper’s criterion, which you will see a little further down.</p>\n<h3>The method, step by step</h3>\n<p>Textbooks divide scientific work into stages. It is not a fixed recipe (Feyerabend will question it shortly), but it shows what is required at each point. Suppose that in a Biology lesson someone claims that Tolosa beans germinate sooner if they are watered with lukewarm water.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Problem</strong>: does the temperature of the water affect germination?</li><li><strong>Systematic observation</strong>: what already happens is carefully recorded (how many days they take, under what conditions).</li><li><strong>Hypothesis</strong>, which must be testable: ‘with water at 25 °C they germinate sooner than with water at 10 °C’.</li><li><strong>Experimentation with control of variables</strong>: two trays identical in everything (soil, light, seeds from the same sack shared out at random) except the temperature of the water, the <strong>independent variable</strong>, the one we change. The days they take to germinate are the <strong>dependent variable</strong>, the one we measure; everything else are <strong>controlled variables</strong>. If one tray were also next to the radiator, we would no longer know what the difference was due to.</li><li><strong>Analysis</strong>: if the result contradicts the prediction, the hypothesis is <strong>refuted</strong>; if it fulfils it, it is <strong>confirmed</strong>, for the time being, and others must be able to repeat it.</li><li><strong>Laws and theories</strong>: what withstands many tests is generalised.</li></ol>\n<p>A <strong>natural law</strong> states a regularity that holds whenever certain conditions are met: at constant temperature, if the volume of a gas is halved, its pressure doubles (Boyle’s law, 1662). It does not command, as the laws passed by Parliament do: it <strong>describes</strong>, and if a well-established fact contradicts it, it is the law that fails. A <strong>theory</strong> <strong>explains</strong> why the laws hold and brings many of them together: kinetic theory explains Boyle’s law because the gas is made of particles that collide with the walls, and in less space they collide more often. ‘It’s only a theory’ means in everyday speech ‘it’s a guess’; in science, a well-tested theory is the most solid thing we have.</p>\n<h3>The problem of induction</h3>\n<p>Laws rest on induction, which only reaches probable conclusions. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/hume\">David Hume</a></strong> went further (1748): what entitles us to pass from the cases we have observed to those we have not? His argument has two steps. <strong>First</strong>, induction is not deduction: from ‘the sun has risen every day’ it does not follow necessarily that it will rise tomorrow, because imagining the opposite involves no contradiction. <strong>Second</strong>, it cannot be justified by experience either: ‘induction has always worked, so it will go on working’ is another induction, which takes as proven precisely what is in question. It is a circle.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>That the sun will not rise to-morrow is no less intelligible a proposition, and implies no more contradiction, than the affirmation, that it will rise.</p><span class=\"per\">— Hume, <em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em> (1748), IV, 1</span></blockquote>\n<p>Why do we go on trusting it? For Hume, out of <strong>habit</strong>: after seeing two facts together many times, the mind expects the second as soon as the first appears; this is not reasoning, but a habit without which we could not live. For centuries Europeans were sure that all swans were white, until in 1697 Willem de Vlamingh’s expedition saw black swans in Australia. Popper will draw his lesson from this: a thousand cases do not prove a law, but a single one can refute it. You will study Hume in Year 2, with <a href=\"#teoria/hf-racionalismo\">empiricism</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: you have always found that water boils at 100 °C… until you climb the Gorbea and it boils at about 95. Was the law wrong, or had you stated it without all its conditions?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-65\">Two 20th-century philosophers renewed this image of science. <strong>Karl Popper</strong> maintained that a theory can never be proved true definitively, it can only withstand attempts to refute it: that is why what distinguishes a scientific claim is its <strong>falsifiability</strong>, that is, that one can conceive of an experiment capable of disproving it. Science would thus advance by trial and error, through conjectures and refutations. For this reason, for Popper, science does not possess the truth: it <strong>approaches</strong> it without ever being able to be sure of having reached it. Truth works as a <strong>regulative idea</strong>, a goal that guides research, and one theory can have more <strong>verisimilitude</strong> than another. <strong>Thomas Kuhn</strong> qualified this by showing that science does not grow only cumulatively: during long periods of <strong>normal science</strong> scientists work within the same shared framework, a <strong>paradigm</strong>, until anomalies accumulate and a phase of <strong>revolutionary science</strong> arrives: a <strong>scientific revolution</strong> replaces it with another (as when Newton’s physics gave way to Einstein’s).</p>\n<h3>Kuhn: how science changes</h3>\n<p><strong><a href=\"#biografias/kuhn\">Thomas S. Kuhn</a></strong> (1922-1996), a physicist turned historian of science, gave <strong>paradigm</strong> two senses in <em>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</em> (1962). In the broad sense, it is what a scientific community shares: laws, instruments, values and which problems are worth pursuing. In the narrow sense, it is the <strong>exemplars</strong>, solved problems that teach you to see new ones ‘as’ those, just as you learn physics by doing the exercises in the textbook. Inside the paradigm, <strong>normal science</strong> is done, which Kuhn compared to solving puzzles: it is taken for granted that there is a solution, and if it does not come out, the researcher is blamed, not the theory.</p>\n<p>A puzzle that will not come out however hard one tries is an <strong>anomaly</strong>; if anomalies pile up or touch the core of the theory, <strong>crisis</strong> arrives. Kuhn’s favourite example is oxygen. In the 18th century combustion was explained by <strong>phlogiston</strong>, a substance that bodies supposedly lost when burning; but some metals, when heated in air, got heavier, not lighter. In 1774 Joseph Priestley obtained a gas in which candles burned more brightly and called it ‘dephlogisticated air’. Antoine Lavoisier saw in it a new element that <strong>combines</strong> with bodies when they burn and called it <strong>oxygen</strong> (1777). It was a <strong>revolution</strong>: no fact was added; what burning means was changed. Priestley died in 1804 without accepting it.</p>\n<p>Hence <strong>incommensurability</strong> (‘no common measure’): two paradigms cannot be compared point by point with a neutral yardstick, because words change their meaning. To ‘burn’ meant for Priestley to give off phlogiston and for Lavoisier to combine with oxygen: in front of the same candle, each saw something different, as in the drawing in which some see a duck and others a rabbit.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>After a revolution, scientists are responding to a different world.</p><span class=\"per\">— Thomas S. Kuhn, <em>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</em> (1962), ch. X</span></blockquote>\n<p>So does science change with fashion? In London (1965), <a href=\"#biografias/popper\">Popper</a> replied that normal science exists but is a <strong>danger</strong>: the good scientist is always critical, including of their own framework; and Imre Lakatos accused Kuhn of reducing theory change to mob psychology. Kuhn denied being a relativist: theories are chosen with shared reasons, such as <strong>accuracy</strong>, <strong>consistency</strong>, <strong>scope</strong>, <strong>simplicity</strong> and <strong>fruitfulness</strong> (1977). But these can clash, and choosing a paradigm is more like a judgement than a calculation. Whether that saves the rationality of science is an <strong>open debate</strong>; you will come back to it in Year 2, with <a href=\"#teoria/hf-analitica\">analytic philosophy</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: in Physics you study Newton even though relativity corrects him. Is that normal science? What would have to happen for today’s textbook to stop being taught?</p>\n<p>Another twentieth-century thinker radicalised the debate in the opposite direction: <strong><a href=\"#biografias/feyerabend\">Paul Feyerabend</a></strong> (1924-1994) rejected the idea that there is <strong>a single method</strong> that science must always follow. On examining specific historical episodes (such as Galileo's commitment to heliocentrism), he observed that decisive advances were often achieved by <strong>breaking</strong> the methodological rules in force, not by respecting them. That is why he launched his provocative slogan, ‘<strong>everything goes</strong>’ (<em>anything goes</em>): not to defend chaos, but to warn that subjecting science to a rigid method would render it sterile. He is the <strong>counterpoint</strong> to Popper: while Popper seeks a criterion that distinguishes science from what is not science, Feyerabend is suspicious of any universal recipe.</p>\n<p>His central case, in <em>Against Method</em> (1975), is Galileo. Against a moving Earth there was an apparently empirical argument: a stone dropped from a tower falls at its foot, not a few metres behind, as it should if the Earth were turning. Galileo did not refute it with new data, but by reinterpreting what everyone saw with a different idea of motion; he defended the telescope when nobody could explain why it should be trusted. Under Popper’s rules, Feyerabend concluded, Copernicus’s theory would have been refuted at birth. In <em>Science in a Free Society</em> (1978) he added that, just as the State was separated from the Church, it should be separated from science, and that citizens, not only experts, should decide what is taught and what is funded.</p>\n<p>Many objected that by putting all traditions on the same level he left medicine defenceless against quackery; Alan Chalmers (1976) added that the fact that methods change over history does not imply that any method is as good as another. Feyerabend explained that ‘anything goes’ was not a principle of his, but the terrified exclamation of a rationalist who looks closely at history. His warning is still useful: be wary of anyone who claims to have the recipe for science.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: does science need a strict method to deserve trust, or does it sometimes progress precisely by breaking it? If “anything goes”, how then do we tell science from pseudoscience? It is an <strong>open debate</strong> that you will continue in Year 2.</p>\n\n<p>Falsifiability also helps to unmask <strong>pseudoscience</strong>, which imitates the appearance of science without accepting its checks. Think of the ‘balance’ bracelets that used to be sold to athletes: in blind tests their effect <strong>could not be repeated</strong>; every failure was covered up with a <strong>tailor-made excuse</strong>; and terms such as ‘energy’ were used <strong>without defining them</strong> or saying <strong>how to measure them</strong>. Their favourite defence was an <strong>appeal to ignorance</strong>: ‘nobody has proved that they don’t work’. But having no evidence against something is not the same as having evidence for it: the burden of proof lies with <strong>the one who makes the claim</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Where is the boundary?</h3>\n<p>Drawing that line is the <strong>demarcation problem</strong>. Falsifiability does not require a theory to have <em>already</em> been tested, but that it <em>can</em> be. In 1916 Einstein predicted <strong>gravitational waves</strong>, so faint that nobody knew how to measure them; the LIGO detectors did not pick them up until 2015. For a century it was a prediction <strong>not yet tested</strong>, but a risky one: it said what would have to be observed, and it could fail. By contrast, ‘everything happens for a reason’ is <strong>irrefutable</strong>: whatever happens, it fits, and that is why it risks nothing. Nor is it decided by who says it: swapping the authority of a priest for that of a white coat does not make a claim scientific; what counts is how it can be checked.</p>\n<p>What is more, nobody observes with empty eyes: what we see depends on the <strong>theoretical framework</strong> from which we look (the <strong>theory-ladenness</strong> of observation, as Norwood R. Hanson put it in 1958). Faced with an X-ray, you see grey patches and the trauma surgeon sees a fracture. The framework also decides what to do when something does not fit. Since the orbit of Uranus did not agree with Newton, Urbain Le Verrier assumed there was another planet and calculated where it would be: in 1846 Neptune was found. With Mercury he tried the same thing and his planet, Vulcan, never appeared; that was explained by relativity in 1915. Saving a theory with an added hypothesis can be a success or an excuse: Imre Lakatos proposed checking whether that hypothesis makes new predictions that come true.</p>\n<h3>Bunge: a list instead of a rule</h3>\n<p>For <strong>Mario Bunge</strong>, falsifiability alone is not enough: astrology makes predictions that fail and goes on being what it is. He proposed looking at the whole <strong>field of knowledge</strong> and compared a science with a <strong>pseudoscience</strong> in this way (‘What is pseudoscience?’, 1984):</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Community</strong>: researchers who criticise one another, as against believers or sellers who do not argue among themselves.</li><li><strong>Object</strong>: real, measurable things, as against undefined entities (‘vital energy’, ‘vibrations’).</li><li><strong>Relation to other sciences</strong>: consistency with what they already know, as against isolation; homeopathy clashes with what chemistry knows about dilutions.</li><li><strong>Method</strong>: repeatable tests, with logic and mathematics, as against tests that count only the hits.</li><li><strong>Aims</strong>: to understand, even if it is applied later, as against looking mainly for customers.</li><li><strong>History</strong>: knowledge that grows and corrects itself, as against static doctrines; homeopathy still essentially follows Samuel Hahnemann’s book (1810).</li></ul>\n<p>A single feature condemns nobody: physics too deals with things that cannot be seen. What gives a pseudoscience away is failing on many at once. In between lie the <strong>protosciences</strong>, fields still looking for their method, like chemistry in the days of alchemy. Try the list on the cases that follow.</p>\n<h3>Pseudosciences today</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Astrology</strong>: in a double-blind experiment published in <em>Nature</em> (Shawn Carlson, 1985), astrologers who had agreed to the conditions had to match birth charts with personality profiles, and they did no better than chance.</li><li><strong>Homeopathy</strong>: in a ‘30C’ preparation the dilution is such that not a single molecule of the original substance remains. An Australian review in 2015 found no condition for which it works better than a placebo. In Spain it does not appear on the first official list of pseudotherapies (2019, 73 techniques): it was left among the techniques ‘under evaluation’, and that report has not been published.</li><li><strong>Flat-earthism</strong>: it ignores what anyone can see; from Zarautz, ships sailing away disappear hull first, and the Earth’s shadow during eclipses of the Moon is always round. Every piece of contrary evidence is put down to a conspiracy.</li><li><strong>Anti-vaccine movement</strong>: in 1998 Andrew Wakefield linked the MMR vaccine to autism in <em>The Lancet</em> on the basis of twelve children. He had manipulated the data; the journal retracted the article in 2010 and he was struck off. A Danish study of more than 650,000 children (2019) found no link, but the fear remained and measles has broken out again.</li><li><strong>‘Quantum mysticism’</strong>: it uses words from physics (‘energy’, ‘vibration’, ‘the observer creates reality’) to sell therapies or positive-thinking courses. In quantum mechanics, ‘observing’ means that a system interacts with a measuring device, not that a mind changes it by wishing hard enough.</li></ul>\n<p>And what science has not yet investigated? The scientific attitude is neither to believe it nor to mock it, but to <strong>suspend judgement</strong> and put it to the test. Willow bark led to aspirin, and the pharmacologist Tu Youyou started from an old text of Chinese medicine to isolate artemisinin against malaria (Nobel Prize, 2015); other remedies did no better than a placebo. In both cases it was the test that decided, not tradition or prejudice. There are also questions that science cannot answer, such as whether there is life after death. Its <strong>methodological naturalism</strong> looks only for natural causes that it knows how to test, and that entitles it neither to affirm nor to deny what lies outside: the fact that something cannot be investigated proves neither those who defend it nor those who deny it right.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: a friend says homeopathy works for him because, since he started taking it, he catches fewer colds. What experiment would you suggest to him to find out whether it is the homeopathy, the placebo effect or chance?</p>\n<table data-bloque=\"fil-t3-69\">\n<thead>\n<tr> <th>Type of science</th> <th>Object of study</th> <th>How it proves</th> <th>Examples</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td><strong>Formal</strong></td> <td>Abstract forms and relations (without empirical content)</td> <td>By logical coherence, without experiments</td> <td>Logic, mathematics</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Empirical – natural</strong></td> <td>The facts and processes of nature</td> <td>Observation and experiment</td> <td>Physics, chemistry, biology</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td><strong>Empirical – social</strong></td> <td>Human behaviour and life in society</td> <td>Observation, data, interpretation</td> <td>History, economics, sociology, psychology</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<h3>What was science, and what was the cosmos, in each era?</h3>\n<h3>Soundness and completeness: what a calculus can prove</h3><p>A <strong>formal calculus</strong> is a set of rules for obtaining formulae from others in a mechanical way. We ask two things of a good calculus. That it be <strong>sound</strong>: that it only prove valid formulae, that is, that it never lead us to prove a falsehood. And that it be <strong>complete</strong>: that it prove <em>all</em> valid formulae, leaving none out. Soundness guarantees that it does not deceive us; completeness, that it does not fall short.</p><p>The truth tables of propositional logic meet both conditions: a formula comes out true in all rows if and only if it is a tautology. Imagine, by contrast, a system invented with a rule that allowed moving from “p” to “not p”. With it we could deduce “p ∧ ¬p”, a contradiction: it would be an unsound calculus, and moreover useless, because from a contradiction anything follows.</p><p>Kurt Gödel proved in his doctoral thesis (1929) that first-order logic, richer than propositional logic, also has a complete calculus. But in 1931, in the article “On formally undecidable propositions of <em>Principia Mathematica</em> and related systems”, he proved something very different: every consistent formal system powerful enough to express arithmetic contains true statements that the system itself cannot prove. This is his <strong>incompleteness theorem</strong>. The two results should not be confused: the first speaks of logic; the second, of the mathematical theories built with it. And adding axioms does not fix anything, because the new system will have its own unprovable statements.</p><p>The consequence is a limit of formalisation: mathematics is not reduced to a machine that fabricates all its truths by applying rules. What that means for the human mind is an open debate: some see there the proof that we do not think like a machine; others reply that we too might be subject to similar limits.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: does Gödel show that human reason has limits, or only the concrete systems that we build with it?</p>\n<p>Neither the word “science” nor the picture of the cosmos has always meant the same thing. For a Greek, science was demonstrated knowledge and the cosmos a closed sphere full of purposes; for Newton, a set of mathematical laws and a great machine; for us, theories that are put to the test and a universe that has been expanding for about 13,800 million years. Each era changed both things at once: <strong>how to know</strong> and <strong>what is out there</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap diag\"><figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 480 754\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"What was the cosmos like? Five pictures: Greece, the Middle Ages, the scientific revolution, the 18th-19th centuries and the 20th-21st centuries\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><title>What was the cosmos like? Five pictures: Greece, the Middle Ages, the scientific revolution, the 18th-19th centuries and the 20th-21st centuries</title><defs><marker id=\"fleras\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-mk\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"4\" y=\"6\" width=\"472\" height=\"142\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-b\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"14\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"26\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"38\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"50\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"58\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"7\" class=\"dg-a\"/><circle cx=\"102.0\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-m\"/><path d=\"M56.4 49.4 L60.4 49.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M54.3 54.3 L57.2 57.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M49.4 56.4 L49.4 60.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M44.5 54.3 L41.6 57.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M42.4 49.4 L38.4 49.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M44.5 44.5 L41.6 41.6\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M49.4 42.4 L49.4 38.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M54.3 44.5 L57.2 41.6\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"49.4\" cy=\"49.4\" r=\"4\" class=\"dg-o\"/><circle cx=\"82.0\" cy=\"125.6\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"133.1\" cy=\"86.1\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"116.3\" cy=\"117.7\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"84.1\" cy=\"133.4\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"48.8\" cy=\"127.2\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"23.9\" cy=\"101.4\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"18.9\" cy=\"65.9\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"35.7\" cy=\"34.3\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"67.9\" cy=\"18.6\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"103.2\" cy=\"24.8\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"128.1\" cy=\"50.6\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"34\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"600\">Ancient Greece</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"54\" class=\"dg-m\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">Aristotle, 4th c. BC</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"80\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">An onion of spheres, closed</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"99\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">and eternal, with the Earth still</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"118\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">at the centre. Below, what changes;</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"137\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">above, the perfect. All has a purpose.</text><rect x=\"4\" y=\"156\" width=\"472\" height=\"142\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-b\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"14\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"26\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"38\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"50\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"58\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"7\" class=\"dg-a\"/><circle cx=\"102.0\" cy=\"226.0\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-m\"/><path d=\"M56.4 199.4 L60.4 199.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M54.3 204.3 L57.2 207.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M49.4 206.4 L49.4 210.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M44.5 204.3 L41.6 207.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M42.4 199.4 L38.4 199.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M44.5 194.5 L41.6 191.6\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M49.4 192.4 L49.4 188.4\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M54.3 194.5 L57.2 191.6\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"49.4\" cy=\"199.4\" r=\"4\" class=\"dg-o\"/><circle cx=\"82.0\" cy=\"275.6\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"133.1\" cy=\"236.1\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"116.3\" cy=\"267.7\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"84.1\" cy=\"283.4\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"48.8\" cy=\"277.2\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"23.9\" cy=\"251.4\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"18.9\" cy=\"215.9\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"35.7\" cy=\"184.3\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"67.9\" cy=\"168.6\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"103.2\" cy=\"174.8\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><circle cx=\"128.1\" cy=\"200.6\" r=\"1.6\" class=\"dg-m\"/><path d=\"M138.0 226.0 L144.0 226.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M133.3 249.7 L138.8 252.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M119.8 269.8 L124.1 274.1\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99.7 283.3 L102.0 288.8\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M76.0 288.0 L76.0 294.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M52.3 283.3 L50.0 288.8\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M32.2 269.8 L27.9 274.1\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M18.7 249.7 L13.2 252.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M14.0 226.0 L8.0 226.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M18.7 202.3 L13.2 200.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M32.2 182.2 L27.9 177.9\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M52.3 168.7 L50.0 163.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M76.0 164.0 L76.0 158.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99.7 168.7 L102.0 163.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M119.8 182.2 L124.1 177.9\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M133.3 202.3 L138.8 200.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"184\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"600\">The Middle Ages</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"204\" class=\"dg-m\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">5th-15th c.</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"230\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">The same spheres, but created</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"249\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">by God, who is beyond</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"268\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">the last one. A hierarchical order:</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"287\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">everything in its place.</text><rect x=\"4\" y=\"306\" width=\"472\" height=\"142\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-b\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"376.0\" r=\"56\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M120.0 376.0 L130.0 376.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M117.6 400.0 L122.8 403.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M100.0 417.6 L103.0 422.8\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M76.0 420.0 L76.0 430.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M52.0 417.6 L49.0 422.8\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M34.4 400.0 L29.2 403.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M32.0 376.0 L22.0 376.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M34.4 352.0 L29.2 349.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M52.0 334.4 L49.0 329.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M76.0 332.0 L76.0 322.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M100.0 334.4 L103.0 329.2\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M117.6 352.0 L122.8 349.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"376.0\" r=\"24.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"376.0\" r=\"38.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/><path d=\"M76 376 L96.0 342.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M76 376 L48.0 384.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"52.0\" cy=\"383.0\" r=\"4\" class=\"dg-a\"/><circle cx=\"95.0\" cy=\"344.0\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-m\"/><path d=\"M86.0 376.0 L90.0 376.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M83.1 383.1 L85.9 385.9\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M76.0 386.0 L76.0 390.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M68.9 383.1 L66.1 385.9\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M66.0 376.0 L62.0 376.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M68.9 368.9 L66.1 366.1\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M76.0 366.0 L76.0 362.0\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M83.1 368.9 L85.9 366.1\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"376.0\" r=\"7\" class=\"dg-o\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"334\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"600\">The scientific revolution</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"354\" class=\"dg-m\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">17th c.</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"380\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">A machine, like a clock:</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"399\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">matter in motion and the same</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"418\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">laws in the heavens and on Earth.</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"437\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">The Sun at the centre; no purposes.</text><rect x=\"4\" y=\"456\" width=\"472\" height=\"142\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-b\"/><path d=\"M42 468 L110 468 M42 584 L110 584\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M48 470 Q48 516 73 526 Q48 536 48 582 L104 582 Q104 536 79 526 Q104 516 104 470 Z\" class=\"dg-g\" stroke=\"var(--muted)\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><path d=\"M60 496 Q76 502 92 496 Q84 514 76 522 Q68 514 60 496 Z\" class=\"dg-o\"/><path d=\"M76 526 L76 566\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke=\"var(--e-ant)\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/><path d=\"M50 580 Q76 552 102 580 Z\" class=\"dg-o\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"484\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"600\">The 18th and 19th centuries</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"504\" class=\"dg-m\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">Enlightenment and positivism</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"530\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">Newton’s clock, stable and perhaps</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"549\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">infinite; but time stretches out:</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"568\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">the Earth and life have a very long</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"587\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">history, and energy runs down.</text><rect x=\"4\" y=\"606\" width=\"472\" height=\"142\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-b\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"676.0\" r=\"34\" class=\"dg-l\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"676.0\" r=\"56\" class=\"dg-l\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/><ellipse cx=\"92.9\" cy=\"669.8\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(20 92.9 669.8)\"/><path d=\"M117.3 661.0 L132.4 655.5\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#fleras)\"/><ellipse cx=\"72.2\" cy=\"654.3\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(100 72.2 654.3)\"/><path d=\"M68.4 632.7 L65.6 616.9\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#fleras)\"/><ellipse cx=\"62.8\" cy=\"671.2\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(160 62.8 671.2)\"/><path d=\"M34.7 661.0 L19.6 655.5\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#fleras)\"/><ellipse cx=\"60.6\" cy=\"694.4\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(230 60.6 694.4)\"/><path d=\"M47.7 709.7 L37.4 722.0\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#fleras)\"/><ellipse cx=\"86.0\" cy=\"693.3\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"2.4\" class=\"dg-a\" transform=\"rotate(300 86.0 693.3)\"/><path d=\"M98.0 714.1 L106.0 728.0\" class=\"dg-l\" marker-end=\"url(#fleras)\"/><circle cx=\"76.0\" cy=\"676.0\" r=\"3\" class=\"dg-o\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"634\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"600\">The 20th and 21st centuries</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"654\" class=\"dg-m\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">relativity and quantum theory</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"680\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">A universe with a beginning, about</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"699\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">13,800 million years ago, which is</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"718\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">expanding. Space and time curve;</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"737\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\">in the very small, all is probable.</text></svg><figcaption>An onion, the same building with God outside, a clock, an hourglass and a balloon being blown up: five pictures of the cosmos</figcaption></figure></div>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Era</th><th>What was called “science”?</th><th>How was it known?</th><th>What was sought?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ancient Greece</td><td><em>Epistēmē</em>: certain, demonstrated knowledge, the opposite of opinion (<em>doxa</em>).</td><td>Observing and, above all, reasoning: demonstrations (the syllogism, Euclid’s geometry). Hardly any experiments.</td><td>The causes of things, including the final one: what each thing is for.</td></tr><tr><td>Middle Ages</td><td><em>Scientia</em>, the Latin translation: demonstrative knowledge. Theology, the highest science.</td><td>Reading, commenting on and debating the authorities (Aristotle, the Bible) at university; some, such as Roger Bacon, defend experience.</td><td>Understanding a world created by God and ordered in a hierarchy.</td></tr><tr><td>Scientific revolution (17th c.)</td><td>“Natural philosophy”, but mathematical and experimental.</td><td>Experiment, measurement and mathematics (Galileo); gathering facts (Francis Bacon); deducing with method (Descartes).</td><td>Laws of nature, without final causes. “Knowledge is power”.</td></tr><tr><td>18th and 19th centuries</td><td>Positive science. In 1834 the English word <em>scientist</em> is coined.</td><td>Verified facts and laws; each science specialises.</td><td>Progress. The method of physics is to be applied to human beings and society too.</td></tr><tr><td>20th and 21st centuries</td><td>Theories that can be tested and corrected.</td><td>Experiments and models, reviewed by a community (Popper, Kuhn).</td><td>Explaining and predicting, knowing that there are limits to what we can understand.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Beware of two simplifications. The Middle Ages were not an age without science: universities were born then, and authors such as Ockham and Buridan debated motion and method. And the scientific revolution did not change everything at a stroke: Kepler cast horoscopes and Newton spent years on alchemy.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: do you think our picture of the cosmos will seem, five hundred years from now, as old as Aristotle’s onion seems to us today? And our idea of science?</p>\n<p>That scientific revolution did not begin with Galileo. It was opened, a century earlier, by <strong><a href=\"#biografias/copernico\">Nicolaus Copernicus</a></strong> (1473-1543). In <em>On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</em> (1543) he dared to change the centre of the universe: no longer the Earth, at rest, but the <strong>Sun</strong>, with the Earth turning around it like one more planet. The change was not only astronomical: it displaced the human being from the centre of the cosmos and forced us to trust <strong>calculation</strong> rather than what the senses “see” (that the Earth is still).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>At the centre of everything resides the Sun. — Nicolaus Copernicus, <em>On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</em> (1543), I, 10</p> </blockquote>\n<p>During the nineteenth century, the philosopher <strong><a href=\"#biografias/comte\">Auguste Comte</a></strong> (1798-1857) radicalised that faith in science with <strong>positivism</strong>: only what can be <strong>verified</strong> against facts deserves to be called knowledge; the rest is worthless metaphysics. According to his <strong>law of the three stages</strong>, humanity has understood the world first through gods (theological stage), then through abstract essences and forces (metaphysical stage) and, finally, through testable laws (positive stage). He also sought to transfer the method of physics to the study of society: that is how <strong>sociology</strong> arose.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Science, hence foresight; foresight, hence action. — Auguste Comte, <em>Course of Positive Philosophy</em> (1830-1842), 2nd lesson</p> </blockquote>\n<p>An example of the three stages. Faced with a storm, the <strong>theological</strong> explanation looks for a will: in Basque mythology, Mari, the lady of Anboto, unleashes hail when she is offended. The <strong>metaphysical</strong> one replaces the gods with abstract entities: a hidden ‘force’ in the air. The <strong>positive</strong> one gives up on the ultimate cause and the ‘what for’, and finds out <strong>how</strong> it happens: which laws link pressure, temperature and humidity, which is what makes it possible to forecast the storm. For Comte, each person repeats this path: a theologian in childhood, a metaphysician in youth and a ‘physicist’ in maturity.</p>\n<p>Comte also arranged the sciences in a <strong>hierarchy</strong>: mathematics, astronomy, physics, chemistry, biology and sociology, from the most general and simple to the most complex; each one needs those before it and reaches the positive stage after them. The last, the science of society, which he first called ‘social physics’ and in 1838 <strong>sociology</strong>, would crown the system.</p>\n<p>Science itself gave him the first reply. In 1835 Comte gave the chemical composition of the stars as an example of what we would never know; in 1859 Gustav Kirchhoff and Robert Bunsen showed that the spectrum of a star’s light reveals its elements, and in 1868 helium was found in the Sun before it was found on Earth. The second reply is philosophical: the law of three stages cannot be verified; it is an interpretation of history, as ‘metaphysical’ as what it opposed. And Comte himself ended up founding a <strong>religion of humanity</strong>: he wanted science to take over the social place of religion as well.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is it true that only what can be verified by facts deserves to be called “knowledge”? Where, then, do ethics, art or philosophy itself stand? You will study Copernicus and Comte in depth in Year 2.</p>\n<p>Ordering and classifying knowledge is an old aspiration. In the heart of the Age of Enlightenment, the mathematician <strong><a href=\"#biografias/dalembert\">Jean le Rond d'Alembert</a></strong> (1717-1783) opened the <em>Encyclopédie</em> with a <strong>Preliminary Discourse</strong> (1751) in which he drew a «map» of all human knowledge, ordered according to three faculties of the mind: memory (history), reason (philosophy and the sciences) and imagination (the arts). It was a way of asserting that knowledge forms a <strong>system</strong> with its branches, and that reason can travel through it all without need of authority.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can all knowledge be ordered in a single scheme, or are there always kinds of knowledge that do not fit? Who decides what goes in each branch? You will meet d'Alembert in Year 2, in the Enlightenment.</p>\n<p>Before Popper, the <strong>Vienna Circle</strong>, led by <strong><a href=\"#biografias/schlick\">Moritz Schlick</a></strong> (1882-1936), proposed another criterion to separate science from nonsense: only what can be <strong>verified</strong> by experience has meaning (<strong>verificationism</strong>). Popper’s falsifiability was born, in part, as a criticism of that idea.</p>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Factual sciences</td><td>For Bunge, those that study facts and test them against experience, as opposed to the formal sciences.</td></tr><tr><td>Paradigm</td><td>For Kuhn, what a scientific community shares and the model problems through which it is learnt.</td></tr><tr><td>Normal science</td><td>Research that solves problems within a paradigm without calling it into question.</td></tr><tr><td>Anomaly</td><td>A result that does not fit the paradigm; if anomalies pile up, they open a crisis.</td></tr><tr><td>Incommensurability</td><td>The lack of a common measure between two paradigms, because their concepts change meaning.</td></tr><tr><td>Protoscience</td><td>A field that aspires to be a science but does not yet have a settled method.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>7. Science, technology, art and society</h2>\n<p>It is common to confuse three different things. <strong>Science</strong> seeks to <em>know</em> reality (to know why things happen). <strong>Technique</strong> is the practical skill for <em>solving</em> problems and making objects; it has existed since human beings carved the first tool, long before science. <strong>Technology</strong>, by contrast, is more recent: it consists in systematically applying <strong>scientific</strong> knowledge to design practical solutions, although the border between technique and technology is not always clear. When science and technology become intertwined to the point of being inseparable, some authors speak of <strong>technoscience</strong>: not a simple sum of the two, but an enormous system of effective actions —based on scientific knowledge— capable of transforming the world, and also a great source of power.</p>\n<p>For the Navarrese philosopher <strong>Javier Echeverría</strong> (<em>La revolución tecnocientífica</em>, 2003), this is today the normal way of doing research: large teams, extremely expensive equipment and funding from companies, governments and armies as much as from universities, with a product or a goal in view from the outset. That is how the messenger RNA vaccines against COVID were achieved in less than a year; but it is also why it matters to know <strong>who decides</strong> what is researched (we saw this in ‘Knowledge, power and interest’).</p>\n<p>Precisely because of that power, science and technology need the reflection of philosophy and ethics. Knowing <em>how</em> to do something is not the same as knowing <em>whether we should</em> do it. The <strong>ethical limits</strong> have become urgent: <strong>climate change</strong> forces us to think about which technologies are sustainable; <strong>artificial intelligence</strong> raises the question of who is responsible for its decisions and which jobs or freedoms are at stake; <strong>genetics</strong> makes it possible to modify living beings —even humans— and confronts us with questions that no formula resolves. Science provides very powerful means, but the <strong>ends</strong> and the values have to be decided with practical reason.</p>\n<p>That said, we have to acknowledge the obvious. In <strong>explaining</strong>, <strong>predicting</strong> and <strong>transforming</strong> nature, science has achieved results that no other form of knowledge has matched. Life expectancy at birth in Spain was about 35 years in 1900 and passed 83 in 2023; smallpox, which in the 20th century alone killed some 300 million people, was declared eradicated in 1980 after a worldwide vaccination campaign; the total solar eclipse of 12 August 2026, which crossed the Iberian Peninsula, had been calculated to the minute decades in advance. No other form of knowledge —neither tradition, nor religion, nor philosophy— has achieved anything comparable in that field. That is why <strong>Bertrand Russell</strong> went so far as to write that what science cannot discover, mankind cannot know (<em>Religion and Science</em>, 1935).</p>\n<p>Others accept those successes and deny the conclusion. <strong>Max Weber</strong>, in the lecture ‘Science as a Vocation’ (delivered in 1917, published in 1919), recalled a question of Tolstoy’s: what shall we do and how shall we live? That question, he said, science does not answer, however much it teaches us to achieve what we want. The results of science are measured in what can be measured; where that is not possible, the comparison loses its meaning, and the very success of science raises questions (what to research, what to do with the atomic bomb or with gene editing) that it cannot settle on its own. Recognising the superiority of science in its own field and denying it a monopoly on knowledge are two compatible theses; the argument is about where that field ends.</p>\n<h3>Is technology neutral?</h3>\n<p><strong>Francis Bacon</strong> presented the new science as power: science and human power ‘coincide’, because whoever knows the causes produces the effects, although nature ‘is only overcome by obeying her’ (<em>Novum Organum</em>, 1620, I, 3). At the end of the work (II, 52) he gave it a religious meaning: through original sin human beings lost their innocence and their dominion over creation; faith repairs the first loss, and the arts and sciences the second. With the climate disrupted, that programme of dominion is now under discussion.</p>\n<p>It is often said that technology is <strong>neutral</strong>: a hammer can hang a picture or hurt someone, and everything depends on the use. Langdon Winner (1980) denied it: artefacts ‘have politics’, because their design fixes who can do what. His example, the low bridges over the Long Island parkways, built to keep out the buses from poor neighbourhoods, has been disputed by Bernward Joerges (1999), but the idea can be seen in present-day cases. An algorithm that rewards humiliating videos because they get more views is not a mere ‘use’: someone chose what to optimise. And a patented seed that cannot be resown decides for the farmer. See it in the ‘Technoethics’ dilemmas: the algorithm one and the seed one.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#biografias/heidegger\">Martin Heidegger</a> (‘The Question Concerning Technology’, 1953) granted that technology is a means to an end, but that, he said, misses what is essential: modern technology is a way of looking in which everything appears as an exploitable <strong>resource</strong>. For the poet, the Rhine is a landscape; for the power station, a flow that supplies energy. Today the same happens to our attention on social media.</p>\n<p><strong>Hans Jonas</strong> (1903-1993) drew an ethics from this (<em>The Imperative of Responsibility</em>, 1979). Traditional ethics looked at the neighbour close at hand and at immediate effects, because our actions reached no further. Modern technology does reach further: nuclear power or the editing of inheritable genes affects those not yet born. So he called for a new imperative and a <strong>heuristics of fear</strong>: faced with the irreversible, give more weight to the bad prognosis than to the good one.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Act so that the effects of your action are compatible with the permanence of genuinely human life on Earth.</p><span class=\"per\">— Hans Jonas, <em>The Imperative of Responsibility</em> (1979), ch. I</span></blockquote>\n<p>Ideas like this inspire the <strong>precautionary principle</strong> of the Rio Declaration (1992): in the face of a serious or irreversible danger, the lack of full scientific certainty is no excuse for not acting. The legal scholar Cass Sunstein (2005) objects that doing nothing also carries risks: holding back a vaccine or a drought-resistant crop can cost lives. Its defenders reply that precaution does not mean banning, but testing and monitoring. And the sea dilemma, with machines that remove plastic and drag in plankton too, asks whether it is better to fix things with more technology or to avoid the damage from the start. Jonas returns in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose an object you use every day (your mobile, an app, the e-scooter). Which decisions made by whoever designed it shape what you do with it? Would you say it is neutral?</p>\n<p>We should also avoid seeing science as the only way of knowing. <strong>Science</strong> and <strong>art</strong> are two different and complementary ways of understanding and creating: science seeks general and objective laws; art expresses the singular, the emotional and the symbolic, and also reveals truths about ourselves that no data can convey. They are not, however, watertight compartments: Greek tragedy seeks the universal through a singular case, and an astronomical observation is always a singular fact. A painting, a novel or a song can teach us as much about the human condition as a treatise.</p>\n<p><strong>Scientism</strong> is another matter: believing that only science knows and that what cannot be measured does not exist or does not matter. Respecting science does not oblige you to embrace scientism; in fact, no experiment proves that thesis. Against that reduction, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/zambrano\">María Zambrano</a></strong> (1904-1991), a disciple of Ortega who lived in exile between 1939 and 1984, proposed in <em>Philosophy and Poetry</em> (1939) a <strong>poetic reason</strong>: a reason that does not give up thinking, but does not leave out what cold concepts cannot reach (feelings, dreams, memory, hope). Philosophy, she said, separated from poetry to seek the universal, and poetry kept what is lived; they must be brought together again.</p>\n<p>An example. Science can describe what happens in the brain of someone who is grieving for their grandmother, and that knowledge helps to treat depression. But someone who has lost a loved one knows something that no brain scan teaches them, and a poem can help them understand it. Beware of the opposite leap: the fact that an intimate experience is legitimate does not make true any belief that comes out of it; if someone claims that their grandmother’s spirit moves objects around the house, that is already a claim about the world and it has to be tested. You will meet Zambrano in Year 2, with <a href=\"#teoria/hf-existencialismo\">existentialism</a>.</p>\n<h3>The paradigm of complexity and the theory of the sign</h3><p>In the twentieth century another image of the universe emerged, the <strong>paradigm of complexity</strong>. In contrast to the predictable machine, the world appears as a <strong>holistic</strong> whole (the parts are understood through their relations), <strong>open</strong>, with <strong>chance</strong>, <strong>irreversible</strong> processes and a capacity for <strong>self-organisation</strong>. Ilya Prigogine, Nobel Prize winner in Chemistry in 1977, and the philosopher Isabelle Stengers proposed in <em>Order out of Chaos</em> (1979) reconciling science and philosophy; Edgar Morin, in <em>Introduction to Complex Thought</em> (1990), argued that no isolated body of knowledge is enough and that disciplines must be integrated. Unlike mechanism, this image leaves room for novelty and, with it, for human freedom and responsibility. Think of the climate or an ecosystem such as that of the Urdaibai estuary: a small change in water temperature or the arrival of a species can trigger effects that no one is able to foresee in detail.</p><p>Understanding such a world requires signs. A <strong>sign</strong> is something that refers to something else. A <strong>signal</strong> has a natural link with what it indicates: smoke with fire; black clouds with rain. A <strong>symbol</strong>, by contrast, is <strong>conventional</strong>, flexible and depends on context: a flag, a word, the black colour of mourning (which in other cultures is white). Ernst Cassirer went so far as to define the human being as a <em>symbolic animal</em> (<em>An Essay on Man</em>, 1944). Charles S. Peirce, whose writings were collected in the <em>Collected Papers</em>, analysed the sign as a three-term relation: the sign, the object to which it refers and the interpretation it produces in someone. And Ferdinand de Saussure, in the <em>Course in General Linguistics</em> (1916), distinguished in the linguistic sign between the <strong>signifier</strong> (the sound image ‘tree’) and the <strong>signified</strong> (the concept).</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: a dog understands that the lead means a walk is coming. Is that a symbol or a signal? Why does it seem that only humans create symbols?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: think of something important that you know and did not learn from any science book. How do you know it? Would you call it ‘knowledge’?</p>\n<p>Lastly, we must not forget the <strong>often invisibilised role of women in science</strong>. For centuries women were kept out of universities and laboratories, and when they did take part, their work was frequently credited to male colleagues. And yet the history of science is full of indispensable names: <strong>Hypatia of Alexandria</strong>, a mathematician and astronomer of antiquity; <strong>Marie Curie</strong>, a pioneer in the study of radioactivity and the only person awarded the Nobel Prize in two different sciences (Physics and Chemistry); or <strong>Rosalind Franklin</strong>, whose images were decisive for discovering the structure of DNA, although her contribution was slow to be recognised. Recovering these names is also a way of knowing better and more justly.</p>\n<h3>Who has been able to do science: barriers with a date</h3>\n<p>The fact that there are few women in the history of science does not prove that they lacked ability: there were <strong>barriers</strong>. <strong>Formal</strong> barriers are written into a rule (a law, a set of statutes) and can be dated. <strong>Informal</strong> ones do not appear on any piece of paper, but they weigh just as much: lack of funding, of posts or of a team willing to take on a woman researcher, prejudices about what is ‘proper’ for a woman. Those have to be measured with data.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Spain</strong>, the university opened up little by little. It is said of <strong>Concepción Arenal</strong> (1820-1893) that around 1841 she attended law classes in Madrid dressed as a man; the story is often repeated, but there are hardly any documents to prove it. What is on record is that in 1872 <strong>María Elena Maseras</strong> enrolled in medicine in Barcelona with express authorisation: she was the first woman enrolled at a Spanish university. Until the <strong>Royal Order of 8 March 1910</strong>, every enrolment by a woman had to be referred to the higher authorities; from then on, women could enrol without asking permission. One of the first to take advantage of this was <strong>Felisa Martín Bravo</strong> (1898-1979), from Donostia, the first woman in Spain to obtain a doctorate in physics (1926) and the first woman in the state meteorological service (1929).</p>\n<p>The <strong>scientific academies</strong> took longer. In 1911 the Académie des sciences in Paris rejected <a href=\"#biografias/curie\">Marie Curie</a>, who already had a Nobel Prize. The Royal Society in London admitted its first women fellows, Kathleen Lonsdale and Marjory Stephenson, in 1945. The Académie elected <strong>Marguerite Perey</strong>, the discoverer of francium, in 1962, and only as a corresponding member. At the Spanish Royal Academy of Exact, Physical and Natural Sciences the first was <strong>Margarita Salas</strong>, in 1988. And this is not just history: since December 2022 the Taliban government has banned women from studying at the universities of <strong>Afghanistan</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The Matilda effect</h3>\n<p>In 1968 the sociologist Robert K. Merton gave the name <strong>Matthew effect</strong> to the tendency to give the credit to whoever is already famous, even when the work is shared (after the Gospel verse ‘to the one who has, more will be given’). In 1993 the historian <strong>Margaret W. Rossiter</strong> showed that this unfair distribution systematically disadvantages women, whose contributions are overlooked or attributed to male colleagues. She called it the <strong>Matilda effect</strong>, after <strong>Matilda Joslyn Gage</strong> (1826-1898), an American suffragist who as early as 1870 denounced the fact that women’s inventions were registered in men’s names. Four much-cited cases:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Nettie Stevens</strong> showed in 1905, using the mealworm, that sex depends on a pair of chromosomes (the ones we now call X and Y). Edmund B. Wilson published a similar result that same year, and many textbooks gave him all the credit.</li><li><strong>Lise Meitner</strong>, an Austrian physicist of Jewish origin who had fled Germany in 1938, explained the results of Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann together with Otto Frisch and called the process <strong>nuclear fission</strong> (1939). The 1944 Nobel Prize in Chemistry went to Hahn alone.</li><li><strong><a href=\"#biografias/franklin\">Rosalind Franklin</a></strong> and her doctoral student Raymond Gosling obtained ‘Photo 51’ of DNA. Maurice Wilkins showed it to James Watson without her knowledge, and it was crucial for the double helix (1953). The 1962 Nobel Prize went to Watson, Crick and Wilkins; Franklin had died in 1958, and the Nobel is not awarded posthumously.</li><li><strong>Jocelyn Bell</strong>, a doctoral student at Cambridge, detected the first pulsar in 1967. The 1974 Nobel Prize in Physics went to her supervisor, Antony Hewish, and to Martin Ryle.</li></ul>\n<p>Careful: the Matilda effect describes a <strong>tendency</strong>; it does not provide a ready-made answer for each case. Bell herself has said that she thought it reasonable for the prize to go to her supervisor, as was customary; and some historians today see Franklin more as an equal collaborator who was later forgotten than as the victim of a theft. Discussing each case with documents is the scientific way of putting the idea to the test.</p>\n<h3>Today’s data</h3>\n<p>According to UNESCO, in 2021 women made up <strong>31.7%</strong> of the world’s research staff. In Spain, the report <em>Científicas en cifras 2025</em> from the Ministry of Science gives a figure of <strong>39.6%</strong> women researchers: 50.5% in public administration, but 31.2% in companies. And the higher the post, the fewer the women: in 2022-2023 they held only 27% of university chairs. This is the so-called <strong>glass ceiling</strong>. In the Basque Country (Euskadi), according to Eustat, in 2024 they made up <strong>37.2%</strong> of R&amp;D staff: 58.7% in administration, 49.5% in higher education and 31.2% in companies.</p>\n<h3>A piece of data is not an explanation</h3>\n<p>A piece of data says <em>what</em> is happening; an explanation says <em>why</em>. That there are fewer women in R&amp;D in Basque companies than at university is a piece of data, compatible with several explanations: that these companies work mainly in engineering, where there are few women graduates (according to UNESCO, women are 28% of engineering graduates worldwide); that stereotypes discourage girls from childhood; or that women are less likely to be hired. A <strong>correlation</strong> (two things that go together) does not prove a <strong>cause</strong>: to decide, more data are needed and, if possible, experiments.</p>\n<p>In the same way, we must distinguish <strong>bias</strong> from <strong>discrimination</strong>. A bias is a tendency, often unconscious, that tilts a judgement; discrimination is unequal and unjust treatment. A bias can discriminate without anyone intending it, and this can be tested. In an experiment published in <em>PNAS</em> in 2012, Corinne Moss-Racusin and her team sent science teaching staff in the United States the same CV, signed sometimes ‘John’ and sometimes ‘Jennifer’. ‘John’ was seen as more competent and offered a higher salary, by men and women staff alike. Since everything else was identical, here we can indeed speak of a cause. More in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-discriminacion\">social discrimination</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: at your school, are there more girls in the Health Sciences track than in the Technology track? That is a piece of data. Suggest three explanations and, for each one, what data or experiment would tell you whether it is true.</p>\n<h2>8. Ortega y Gasset: perspectivism and the triangle of knowledge</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t3-90\">Let us return to the starting point of the topic: to know is a <strong>relation</strong> between a <strong>subject</strong> and an <strong>object</strong>. The Spanish philosopher <strong>José Ortega y Gasset</strong> (1883-1955) added a third element to that scheme. Between the subject and the object there is never a direct, bare contact: the <strong>representation</strong> —the idea, the word, the concept— always comes between them, the representation with which the subject grasps the object from the place it occupies. That is why his theory of knowledge is usually drawn as a <strong>triangle</strong>: at one vertex the subject, at another the object and at the third the idea or representation that puts them in relation. No one knows ‘from nowhere’: one always knows from a particular position.</p>\n<p>That position is what Ortega called <strong>circumstance</strong>: the sum of what surrounds us and has made us who we are (the age, the language, the body, history, the place). Hence his best-known sentence, with which he opens his first book:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>I am myself and my circumstance, and if I do not save it, I do not save myself. — José Ortega y Gasset, <em>Meditations on Quixote</em> (1914)</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Ortega’s proposal is <strong>perspectivism</strong>: each subject contemplates reality from their own perspective, and that perspective is not a hindrance that distorts the truth, but the <strong>organ</strong> with which they grasp a real part of the world that from another point could not be seen. Reality has, so to speak, as many faces as there are points of view on it; no perspective is false for being partial, just as a photograph of a landscape does not lie for being taken from one angle.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Each life is a point of view on the universe; the full truth would be reached only by bringing together what each one sees from their own place. — a paraphrase of José Ortega y Gasset, <em>The Modern Theme</em> (1923), ch. X, ‘The doctrine of the point of view’</p> </blockquote>\n<p>This way of thinking is part of a broader project that Ortega called <strong>ratiovitalism</strong>: neither the pure abstract reason of rationalism (which forgets that the one who reasons is someone alive, set within a circumstance) nor the pure vitalism that gives up reason, but <strong>vital reason</strong>, a reason that thinks <em>from within</em> life and gives an account of it. To know is not the task of a subject with no body and no history, but of a particular person who lives in a specific time and place.</p>\n<p>It is worth not confusing perspectivism with relativism, which we saw in section 3. Here is the most common mistake:</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Trick case: ‘since everyone sees things their own way, all opinions are worth the same and the truth is whatever each person takes it to be’. That is <strong>relativism</strong>, and it is exactly what Ortega <em>denies</em>. For the relativist there is not one truth but many incompatible truths, and it makes no difference which one you choose. For Ortega there is <strong>a single</strong> truth, the same for all; what changes is that each perspective grasps a real part of it, and the perspectives do not contradict one another, but <strong>add up</strong>. That is why it makes sense to compare points of view: not to settle for any one of them, but so that together we may come closer to the complete truth. Relativism gives up on truth; perspectivism seeks it among several.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Perspectivism</strong> (Ortega)</th><th><strong>Relativism</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Is there one truth?</td><td>Yes, a single one, the same for all</td><td>No: there are many truths, according to the subject or the culture</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The point of view</td><td>Grasps a <strong>real</strong> part of the truth</td><td>Locks you into your own opinion; there is no way out</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What to do with other points of view?</td><td>Add them up to come closer to the complete truth</td><td>They make no difference: none is better than another</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Risk</td><td>Believing you already have the total view</td><td>If ‘anything goes’, no lie can be criticised</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2>9. Knowing so as not to be deceived</h2>\n<p>We have travelled a long way: from the question of what it is to know, to the way science and technology transform our world. At bottom, the whole topic was answering one and the same concern: what are we capable of when we know, and how far can we trust what we believe we know?</p>\n<h3>Science and philosophy: two ways of asking questions</h3><p>Science and philosophy were born together, but today they ask in different ways. <strong>Science</strong> studies observable phenomena with precise methods to <strong>explain</strong>, <strong>predict</strong> and, often, <strong>control</strong>. Its problems admit of solutions, even if partial and revisable: we know how long a stone takes to fall, and if an experiment were to qualify that tomorrow, we would correct the calculation. <strong>Philosophy</strong>, by contrast, asks about the totality of what is real, about meaning and about truth. It also has an approach usually called <strong>Transcendental</strong>: it does not confine itself to knowing, but asks about the <em>conditions of possibility</em> of knowledge and action. For that reason its problems remain open.</p><p>Drop a stone from the window. Physics explains its fall by gravity and calculates its trajectory and speed. Philosophy, faced with the same stone, asks other things: what does it mean to ‘explain’ something? Are there really laws in nature or only regularities we have observed? Is everything determined, including what I decide to do with the next stone? No measurement answers that, but without those questions we would not properly know what science does.</p><p>Between the two lies <strong>philosophy of nature</strong>: it reflects on the origin, development and future of the universe, drawing on what science says but seeking a global explanation. It is, in fact, the oldest form of philosophy. The Presocratics thought of <em>phýsis</em> as a <em>kósmos</em>, an ordered whole that reason can understand: Thales sought its principle in water, Anaximander in the indeterminate (<em>apeiron</em>) and Heraclitus in a <em>Lógos</em> that governs ceaseless change.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: in 1714 Leibniz asked why there is something rather than nothing. Can science answer that question on its own, or does it belong to another kind of knowledge?</p>\n<p>One possible and reasonable answer lies as far from the dogmatism of someone who takes everything as certain as from the radical scepticism of someone who gives up searching. It is a critical and humble attitude: examining the sources of our knowledge, distinguishing <strong>opinion</strong> from <strong>knowledge</strong>, demanding proof, checking, and always being ready to correct ourselves. It is not the obligatory conclusion of the topic, but it has good reasons in its favour. In an age of <strong>post-truth</strong>, hoaxes and echo chambers, that attitude is not an academic luxury but a condition of <strong>freedom</strong>: whoever knows how to think for themselves is much harder to manipulate.</p>\n<h3>Check before you believe: tools</h3>\n<p>Knowing that hoaxes exist is not enough; you need a method. Sam Wineburg and Sarah McGrew (Stanford University, 2019) asked historians with doctorates, Stanford students and professional fact-checkers to judge the reliability of several websites. The first two groups read <strong>vertically</strong>: they stayed on the page, examined the design, the ‘about us’ section and the references, and were often fooled by a serious-looking site. The fact-checkers read <strong>laterally</strong>: within seconds they opened other tabs to find out what <em>others</em> said about that site and who was behind it. They read less and got it right more often, and sooner.</p>\n<p>Mike Caulfield, a digital literacy researcher who would later write the book <em>Verified</em> (2023) with Wineburg, summed it up in four steps, <strong>SIFT</strong>:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Stop</strong> (<em>Stop</em>). If a message stirs a strong emotion or an urge to share, pause: do you know the source? What do you want to check?</li><li><strong>Investigate the source</strong> (<em>Investigate the source</em>). Do not ask what it says about itself, but what others say about it: is it a news outlet, a private individual, a company, a campaign?</li><li><strong>Find better coverage</strong> (<em>Find better coverage</em>). Forget the particular message and look for the claim: do reliable outlets or fact-checkers report it? What do the responsible bodies say?</li><li><strong>Trace claims to the original context</strong> (<em>Trace claims to the original context</em>). Find where the quotation, the figure or the image comes from (a reverse image search helps): often they are real, but from another place, another date, or cropped.</li></ol>\n<p>For the third step there are <strong>fact-checkers</strong> (in English, <em>fact-checkers</em>): in Spain, <strong>Maldita.es</strong> (on Twitter since 2014 and a news outlet in its own right since 2018), <strong>Newtral</strong> (2018), <strong>EFE Verifica</strong> (2019, from the EFE agency) or <strong>Verificat</strong> (2019), in Catalonia. Those who sign the code of principles of the International Fact-Checking Network (IFCN, 2016) commit themselves to being non-partisan, to publishing their sources, funding and methods, and to correcting their mistakes openly. They too are disputed: some accuse them of choosing what to check with a political bias, and in January 2025 Meta announced that it would stop paying them in the United States; since April, Community Notes written by users themselves have replaced them there. Who checks the checker is an <strong>open debate</strong>; that is why it is wise to compare fact-checkers with one another too, and not to hand your own judgement over to any of them.</p>\n<p><strong>A case for class</strong> (made up). An audio message ‘forwarded many times’ reaches Irati’s family group: a voice claiming to be ‘a cousin who works at the Water Consortium’ warns that tomorrow the water will be cut off throughout Bilbao because of contamination the government is hiding, and asks people to fill their baths and forward the message. With SIFT: <strong>stop</strong>, because the message stirs fear and urgency, and that WhatsApp label shows it was not written by a close contact; <strong>investigate the source</strong>: there is no name, position or document, only ‘a cousin’; <strong>find better coverage</strong>: such a cut would appear on the website of the Bilbao Bizkaia Water Consortium, in the local media and with the fact-checkers; <strong>trace</strong>: if you search for word-for-word phrases from the audio, the same text may turn up months earlier with the name of another city, a sign of a hoax template. Finally, notice the punchline: ‘it won’t be on the news, because they’re covering it up’. That is conspiratorial self-sealing: the message inoculates itself in advance against checking.</p>\n<p>In that spirit, the best way to close is the motto with which Kant summed up the Enlightenment, that invitation to make use of one’s own understanding without letting others think for us:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Sapere aude. (Dare to know!) — Immanuel Kant, <em>An Answer to the Question: What Is Enlightenment?</em> (1784)</p> </blockquote>\n<p>But be careful not to misread that motto. A sociologist interviewed by Verificat in the report mentioned above notes that many conspiracists practise an individualistic <em>sapere aude</em>: they see themselves as ‘researchers’ who think for themselves, as opposed to the ‘herd’ that believes institutions and science. That is not what Kant was asking for. For him, the public use of reason takes place before others, exposing one’s arguments to their criticism; and in the <em>Critique of Judgement</em> he completed ‘think for yourself’ with two further maxims:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>1. To think for oneself. 2. To think from the standpoint of everyone else. 3. To think always consistently with oneself. <span class=\"per\">— Immanuel Kant, <em>Critique of Judgement</em> (1790), § 40 (our own translation)</span></p></blockquote>\n<p>Someone who keeps only the first maxim is not enlightened but isolated: thinking for yourself also demands testing your own views against other people’s points of view and not contradicting yourself, for instance by accepting without evidence what you like and demanding impossible evidence for what you do not. Autonomy does not consist in trusting nobody, but in telling reasonable trust from blind trust. You will find the motto discussed in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-kant\">Kant</a>, in History of Philosophy.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason, in public and in private.</strong> In that same article of 1784, Kant distinguishes two uses of reason. The <strong>private use</strong> is the one we make in an office or in a post entrusted to us: the officer who receives an order, the citizen who pays his taxes or the parish priest who teaches according to his Church must fulfil their function, and there freedom may be limited. The <strong>public use</strong> is the one anyone makes as a person who reasons before the whole reading public: the same officer can write afterwards about the faults of the service, and the same citizen can publish why a tax seems unjust to him. That use, says Kant, must always be free, because it is the only one that can bring about the Enlightenment. Be careful with the names: 'private' does not mean intimate, but tied to a post; and 'public' does not mean official, but addressed to everyone. This is the Kant text you will read in Year 2 for the PAU.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: Enlightenment thinkers believed that, if schooling reached everyone, superstitions would disappear. Today almost all of us have been to school, and hoaxes keep circulating. Has the Enlightenment failed, or is it a project still open? What would it take, besides information, to think better: time, permission to doubt, trust in others?</p>\n<p>Knowing in order to understand, and understanding in order not to be deceived: that is what it means to go on practising, in this topic too, the <strong>philosophical attitude</strong> with which we began the course.</p>"}});
